WEBVTT

00:00:00.570 --> 00:00:18.700
<v Ira Glass>OK, so one of my co-workers, David Kestenbaum, started telling me about this thing. And really, I thought he was completely-- I just-- there was no way it could be true. It just seemed completely ridiculous. And then, I got to say, the more he explained, the more it slowly started to seem like, OK, maybe he's right.
<v Ira Glass>好的，我的一个同事大卫·克 斯滕鲍姆（David Kestenbaum）开始跟我说这件事。说实话，我当时觉得他完全——我只是——这根本不可能。这听起来太荒谬了。但后来我得说，他解释得越多，我就越觉得，好吧，也许他是对的。

00:00:18.700 --> 00:00:34.000
<v Ira Glass>Important fact to know about David before we go any further-- he has a PhD in physics, worked at Fermilab on subatomic particles, was on the team that discovered the top quark. And then this whole thing between him and me began when he told me to download an app onto my iPhone.
<v Ira Glass>在我们继续之前，有一件关于大卫的重要事实需要了解——他拥有物理学博士学位，曾在费米实验室研究亚原子粒子，是发现顶夸克的团队成员之一。后来，我和他之间的这一切始于他让我下载一个 iPhone 应用程序。

00:00:34.000 --> 00:00:38.150
<v David Kestenbaum>It's called Universe Splitter.
<v David Kestenbaum>它叫“宇宙分裂器”（Universe Splitter）。

00:00:38.150 --> 00:00:39.480
<v David Kestenbaum>[DING]
<v David Kestenbaum>[叮]

00:00:39.480 --> 00:00:54.210
<v Ira Glass>OK, so now I've opened it up. And there's, like, a gray steel, fake steel background. And it says in white type on top of it, Universe Splitter, quantum-induced universe bifurcation. What do I do now?
<v Ira Glass>好了，我现在打开它了。它有一个灰色的、钢质的，假钢质背景。顶部的白色字体写着：宇宙分裂器，量子诱导的宇宙分叉。我现在该做什么？

00:00:54.210 --> 00:01:05.150
<v David Kestenbaum>All right, so tell me something you are having trouble making up your mind about what to do.
<v David Kestenbaum>好的，那么告诉我一件你正在为难、难以做决定该怎么做的事情。

00:01:05.150 --> 00:01:07.690
<v Ira Glass>You and I are recording this on December 31.
<v Ira Glass>我们是在 12 月 31 日录制这期节目的。

00:01:07.690 --> 00:01:08.380
<v David Kestenbaum>Yeah.
<v David Kestenbaum>是的。

00:01:08.380 --> 00:01:10.450
<v Ira Glass>And I've had a week off. I grew a beard.
<v Ira Glass>我休了一周假。我留了胡子。

00:01:10.450 --> 00:01:12.570
<v David Kestenbaum>I noticed.
<v David Kestenbaum>我注意到了。

00:01:12.570 --> 00:01:19.210
<v Ira Glass>And I'm trying to decide if I should shave it off. And so that's something I'm trying to decide.
<v Ira Glass>我正在考虑要不要把它刮掉。这就是我正在纠结的事情。

00:01:19.210 --> 00:01:21.010
<v David Kestenbaum>I have an opinion on that, but--
<v David Kestenbaum>对此我有个看法，但是——

00:01:21.010 --> 00:01:21.990
<v Ira Glass>What's your opinion?
<v Ira Glass>你有什么看法？

00:01:21.990 --> 00:01:25.250
<v David Kestenbaum>[LAUGHS] Actually, I think it looks pretty good right now.
<v David Kestenbaum>[笑] 其实，我觉得你现在的样子挺好的。

00:01:25.250 --> 00:01:28.570
<v Ira Glass>OK, so what does this Universe Splitter do?
<v Ira Glass>好的，那么这个“宇宙分裂器”有什么用？

00:01:28.570 --> 00:01:39.320
<v David Kestenbaum>It lets you do both. Like, it creates a duplicate of this universe so that in one you get to grow the beard, and in the other you shave it off.
<v David Kestenbaum>它让你两者兼得。比如，它创造了这个宇宙的副本，在一个宇宙里你留胡子，在另一个宇宙里你把它刮掉。

00:01:39.320 --> 00:01:40.610
<v Ira Glass>Wait, that's what we're going to do?
<v Ira Glass>等等，这就是我们要做的吗？

00:01:40.610 --> 00:01:41.620
<v David Kestenbaum>Yeah.
<v David Kestenbaum>是的。

00:01:41.620 --> 00:01:42.770
<v Ira Glass>For pretend or for real?
<v Ira Glass>是假装的，还是真的？

00:01:42.770 --> 00:01:51.800
<v David Kestenbaum>No, no, for real, maybe. This is a thing. It's called the many-worlds interpretation of quantum mechanics. And there are a lot of really smart physicists who think this is probably what's happening.
<v David Kestenbaum>不，不，可能是真的。这是一件事。它被称为量子力学的多世界诠释。有很多非常聪明的物理学家认为这很可能正在发生。

00:01:51.800 --> 00:01:54.890
<v Ira Glass>OK, so how does this work? Like, what do I do next?
<v Ira Glass>好的，那这怎么运作？我下一步该做什么？

00:01:54.890 --> 00:01:57.680
<v David Kestenbaum>All right, so there are two boxes there?
<v David Kestenbaum>好了，那里有两个框，对吧？

00:01:57.680 --> 00:02:02.690
<v Ira Glass>Yeah. In one box it says, In one universe, I will now.
<v Ira Glass>是的。其中一个框写着：“在其中一个宇宙中，我现在将……”

00:02:02.690 --> 00:02:05.930
<v David Kestenbaum>OK, so put, shave off beard tonight.
<v David Kestenbaum>好，那就写：今晚刮掉胡子。

00:02:05.930 --> 00:02:19.840
<v Ira Glass>Hold on-- shave off beard tonight. OK, and then the other box is labeled, In the other universe. In the other one, I will now--
<v Ira Glass>等等——今晚刮掉胡子。好的，然后另一个框标记为：“在另一个宇宙中”。在另一个宇宙里，我现在将……

00:02:19.840 --> 00:02:21.980
<v David Kestenbaum>Put keep beard.
<v David Kestenbaum>写：保留胡子。

00:02:21.980 --> 00:02:25.150
<v Ira Glass>Keep beard.
<v Ira Glass>保留胡子。

00:02:25.150 --> 00:02:27.480
<v David Kestenbaum>OK. And then what's below it?
<v David Kestenbaum>好的。那下面是什么？

00:02:27.480 --> 00:02:31.690
<v Ira Glass>And then below it there's like a button with like, an atom drawn on it.
<v Ira Glass>下面有一个像按钮的东西，上面画着一个原子。

00:02:31.690 --> 00:02:32.580
<v David Kestenbaum>[LAUGHS] Yeah.
<v David Kestenbaum>[笑] 是的。

00:02:32.580 --> 00:02:36.570
<v Ira Glass>Like an atom like you would draw it in a 1950s cartoon.
<v Ira Glass>就像你在 1950 年代的卡通片里画的那种原子。

00:02:36.570 --> 00:02:40.680
<v David Kestenbaum>Yeah, that's like the aesthetic of the whole thing. It's like an old piece of scientific equipment.
<v David Kestenbaum>是的，那就是整个东西的美学。它就像一件旧的科学仪器。

00:02:40.680 --> 00:02:43.330
<v Ira Glass>So what does it do when you push the button?
<v Ira Glass>那你按下按钮它会做什么？

00:02:43.330 --> 00:03:08.280
<v David Kestenbaum>So if you push the button, it sends a signal to a fancy piece of scientific equipment at the University of Geneva in Switzerland. And the equipment these days can be tiny, like a little box you can hold in your hand. And it does the following. It takes a single particle of light, a photon, it sends it at a kind of mirror that can make the photon either go left or right. You can think of it that way.
<v David Kestenbaum>如果你按下按钮，它会向位于瑞士日内瓦大学的一台精密科学设备发送信号。如今这台设备可以做得很小，就像你可以拿在手里的小盒子一样。它做的事情如下：它取一个单一的光粒子，一个光子，把它发送到一个可以使光子向左或向右偏转的镜子上。你可以这样想。

00:03:08.280 --> 00:03:10.860
<v Ira Glass>Like at random it'll go either left or right?
<v Ira Glass>就像随机地它会向左或向右？

00:03:10.860 --> 00:03:24.050
<v David Kestenbaum>Well, according to the laws of quantum mechanics, which govern very small things, it actually goes both. It's not that we don't know. It actually goes left and right at the same time.
<v David Kestenbaum>嗯，根据支配微小事物的量子力学定律，它实际上是向两个方向都去了。不是我们不知道，它实际上同时向左和向右去了。

00:03:24.050 --> 00:03:25.230
<v Ira Glass>OK.
<v Ira Glass>好的。

00:03:25.230 --> 00:03:33.120
<v David Kestenbaum>So that's super weird, right? But that is actually true, and demonstrated, and like we've known that since the '20s.
<v David Kestenbaum>所以这超级奇怪，对吧？但这确实是真的，而且经过了验证，我们从 20 年代起就知道这一点了。

00:03:33.120 --> 00:03:36.680
<v Ira Glass>OK. So then what happens in this device in Geneva?
<v Ira Glass>好的。那么这个日内瓦的设备里发生了什么？

00:03:36.680 --> 00:04:01.020
<v David Kestenbaum>Yeah, so the device fires a photon which can go left or right. And then the device looks to see, well, where did it go? And we know the particle's in both places, right? But when the machine looks, it only finds it in one place. It shows it went either left or right, which doesn't make sense, right? Because we know from the math that the particle did go to both places. So why did we only see it in one of those places?
<v David Kestenbaum>是的，设备发射一个可以向左或向右的光子。然后设备观察它去了哪里。我们知道粒子在两个地方，对吧？但当机器观察时，它只在其中一个地方找到了它。它显示它要么向左要么向右，这说不通，对吧？因为我们从数学上知道粒子确实去了两个地方。那么为什么我们只在其中一个地方看到了它？

00:04:01.020 --> 00:04:02.210
<v Ira Glass>And what's the answer?
<v Ira Glass>答案是什么？

00:04:02.210 --> 00:04:11.710
<v David Kestenbaum>All right, so one of the answers is that the photon is in both places, left and right, but just in different universes.
<v David Kestenbaum>好吧，其中一个答案是光子确实在两个地方，左边和右边，只是在不同的宇宙里。

00:04:11.710 --> 00:04:25.860
<v Ira Glass>Wait. So you're saying that when you shoot the photon into the mirror, it actually creates an entire duplicate of our universe. And in one of those universes, the photon is on the left, and the other it is on the right?
<v Ira Glass>等等。你是说当你把光子射入镜子时，它实际上创造了一个我们宇宙的完整副本？在一个宇宙里光子在左边，在另一个宇宙里它在右边？

00:04:25.860 --> 00:04:40.730
<v David Kestenbaum>Yes. It both went left and right. Those are just in different universes. The math of it makes a lot of-- the math of it is very, like, streamlined and simple. I remember the day I saw it in class, and I was like, oh, my god. Maybe it's true.
<v David Kestenbaum>是的。它既向左又向右。它们只是在不同的宇宙里。它的数学逻辑非常有……它的数学非常精简且简单。我记得我在课堂上看到它的时候，我当时想，天哪。也许这是真的。

00:04:40.730 --> 00:04:42.020
<v Ira Glass>Yeah.
<v Ira Glass>是的。

00:04:42.020 --> 00:04:44.390
<v David Kestenbaum>So those boxes you filled out on the app--
<v David Kestenbaum>那么你在应用里填写的那些框——

00:04:44.390 --> 00:04:49.580
<v Ira Glass>You mean the box where I typed in "shave off beard tonight", and the box where I typed in, keep beard?
<v Ira Glass>你是说我输入“今晚刮掉胡子”的那个框，还有我输入“保留胡子”的那个框？

00:04:49.580 --> 00:04:56.660
<v David Kestenbaum>Right, right. So it'll basically choose one of those boxes if the photon goes left, and the other box if the photon goes right.
<v David Kestenbaum>对，对。所以它基本上会选择其中一个框，如果光子向左；选择另一个框，如果光子向右。

00:04:56.660 --> 00:04:57.410
<v Ira Glass>OK, got it.
<v Ira Glass>好的，懂了。

00:04:57.410 --> 00:04:59.360
<v David Kestenbaum>You have to do what it says.
<v David Kestenbaum>你必须照它说的做。

00:04:59.360 --> 00:05:02.450
<v Ira Glass>You mean I have to keep the beard or shave off the beard like it tells me?
<v Ira Glass>你是说我必须保留胡子或刮掉胡子，按照它告诉我的那样？

00:05:02.450 --> 00:05:24.290
<v David Kestenbaum>Yeah. Yeah, because when you press that button, you're going to get back one answer. But there's going to be a duplicate universe in which there is a duplicate you sitting in a duplicate studio and a duplicate me and you holding a duplicate phone in exactly the same way. The only difference is that that phone comes back with the other answer.
<v David Kestenbaum>是的。是的，因为当你按下那个按钮时，你会得到一个结果。但会有一个副本宇宙，里面有一个副本“你”坐在一个副本录音棚里，有一个副本“我”，而且你以同样的方式拿着一部副本手机。唯一的区别是那部手机显示的是另一个结果。

00:05:24.290 --> 00:05:28.470
<v Ira Glass>And then that version of me will do whatever it says on the phone?
<v Ira Glass>然后那个版本的我会做手机上显示的任何事情？

00:05:28.470 --> 00:05:32.040
<v David Kestenbaum>We know that other guy. We trust him. He's going to go do the other.
<v David Kestenbaum>我们认识那个人。我们信任他。他会去执行另一个选项。

00:05:32.040 --> 00:05:36.260
<v Ira Glass>OK, I think I understand all this. Let's go ahead.
<v Ira Glass>好的，我想我明白了。我们开始吧。

00:05:36.260 --> 00:05:38.290
<v David Kestenbaum>Do you want to press the button?
<v David Kestenbaum>你想按那个按钮吗？

00:05:38.290 --> 00:05:40.910
<v Ira Glass>Yeah, go ahead. Push it.
<v Ira Glass>是的，开始吧。按下去。

00:05:40.910 --> 00:05:45.520
<v David Kestenbaum>[LAUGHS] I do feel weird about it. I feel weird. I know it's crazy. I feel weird.
<v David Kestenbaum>[笑] 我对此确实感觉很奇怪。我觉得很奇怪。我知道这很疯狂。我觉得很奇怪。

00:05:45.520 --> 00:05:49.370
<v Ira Glass>You literally just put your finger right up to the button and pulled it away in fear.
<v Ira Glass>你刚才真的把手指放到按钮前，又因为恐惧缩了回去。

00:05:49.370 --> 00:05:53.070
<v David Kestenbaum>All right, here it goes-- the split universe.
<v David Kestenbaum>好了，这就是它了——分裂的宇宙。

00:05:53.070 --> 00:05:54.940
<v David Kestenbaum>[BEEP]
<v David Kestenbaum>[哔]

00:05:54.940 --> 00:06:01.970
<v Ira Glass>OK, it says, Input valid. Internet contacted. Geneva online. Device ready. Photon emitted. Quantum event.
<v Ira Glass>好的，它显示：输入有效。已联系互联网。日内瓦在线。设备就绪。光子已发射。量子事件。

00:06:01.970 --> 00:06:05.300
<v Ira Glass>[ROCKET BLASTING]
<v Ira Glass>[火箭发射声]

00:06:05.300 --> 00:06:20.250
<v Ira Glass>Your universe has just split. You're in the universe in which you should keep beard. And right now in the other universe, the other you is being told to shave off beard tonight.
<v Ira Glass>你的宇宙刚刚分裂了。你处在应该保留胡子的宇宙中。而在另一个宇宙里，另一个你正被告知今晚要刮掉胡子。

00:06:20.250 --> 00:06:45.180
<v Ira Glass>So after that, I kept the beard that night. And friends at New Year's parties asked me about it. And if the universe did in fact split, the other version of me in the other universe must have had different conversations. Maybe for instance somebody gave him a great idea for a show today that, in that universe, instead of airing this show, in that universe you're hearing a totally different theme.
<v Ira Glass>所以在那之后，那天晚上我留下了胡子。新年派对上的朋友们问我这件事。如果宇宙确实分裂了，另一个宇宙里的另一个版本的我一定进行过不同的对话。也许有人给了他一个很棒的节目点子，以至于在那个宇宙里，代替播出这个节目，你在那个宇宙听到的完全是另一个主题。

00:06:45.180 --> 00:06:52.970
<v Ira Glass>And David, just to be sure I'm understanding this, you're saying this isn't a metaphor. You're saying there are scientists who really believe that a second universe gets created?
<v Ira Glass>大卫，为了确保我理解正确，你是说这不仅仅是个比喻。你是说有科学家真的相信一个新的宇宙被创造出来了？

00:06:52.970 --> 00:07:00.600
<v David Kestenbaum>Yeah, for sure. I mean, I think it's not most physicists. And there are some who think it's ridiculous, for sure. But there are some would take it very seriously.
<v David Kestenbaum>是的，肯定的。我的意思是，我不认为这是大多数物理学家的观点。确实有一些人认为这很荒谬。但也有一些人非常认真地对待它。

00:07:00.600 --> 00:07:04.640
<v Ira Glass>OK, where is this alternate universe that we're creating?
<v Ira Glass>好的，我们创造的这个替代宇宙在哪里？

00:07:04.640 --> 00:07:13.870
<v David Kestenbaum>Yeah, I don't really even know how to think about this. But if this is true, the universe is duplicating itself all the time. It's like a fundamental thing about existence.
<v David Kestenbaum>是的，我甚至不知道该怎么去思考它。但如果这是真的，宇宙一直在自我复制。这就像是关于存在的一个基本事实。

00:07:13.870 --> 00:07:48.230
<v Ira Glass>Well, today on our program, in a universe that may be duplicating itself all the time, we have people who have become entranced by the idea of a parallel universe out there where things are different. In one of those stories, things are better in the alternate world in one crucial way. In another story, two parallel worlds overlap. In another, a woman tries to visit-- she actually tries to go to the parallel universe. From WBEZ Chicago, it's This American Life. I'm Ira Glass. Stay with us.
<v Ira Glass>好了，今天在我们的节目里，在一个可能一直在自我复制的宇宙中，我们有一些被平行宇宙的想法所吸引的人，在那里事物是不同的。在其中一个故事里，替代世界在某个关键方面要更好。在另一个故事里，两个平行世界重叠了。在另一个故事里，一个女人试图去访问——她真的试图进入那个平行宇宙。来自芝加哥 WBEZ，这是《美国生活》（This American Life）。我是艾拉·格拉斯（Ira Glass）。请继续收听。

00:07:48.230 --> 00:08:19.220
<v Ira Glass>Act 1, Dreams From My Father. So I was e-mailing with a fiction writer that we have on our show sometimes, Etgar Keret, about today's theme. And he told me that his father used to spend a lot of time imagining worlds like we're talking about in today's program-- parallel worlds to ours that are almost the same as our world, but not quite. And he said his father did this starting during World War II. His father was 14, living in Poland-- he was Jewish-- when the Germans invaded and started sending Jews to the concentration camps.
<v Ira Glass>第一幕，《我父亲的梦》。我曾和节目里常客、小说家埃特加·凯雷特（Etgar Keret）就今天的主题通过邮件交流。他告诉我，他的父亲以前常花很多时间想象我们今天节目里说的这种世界——那些与我们平行、几乎一样但又不完全一样的世界。他说他父亲从二战时期就开始这么做了。他父亲当时 14 岁，住在波兰——他是犹太人——当德国人入侵并开始把犹太人送往集中营时。

00:08:19.220 --> 00:08:32.370
<v Etgar Keret>During the Second World War, him and his parents, they hid in a hole in the ground that they dug for 620 days. So he had a lot of spare time.
<v Etgar Keret>二战期间，他和他的父母躲在他们挖的一个地洞里，躲了 620 天。所以他有很多空余时间。

00:08:32.370 --> 00:08:56.460
<v Etgar Keret>And when he was there, he would imagine all kinds of things. He would imagine a world in which the Nazis didn't exist, or in which he wasn't a Jew, or in which people just in general didn't kill each other, you know? And this kind of thing was something that he associated with being a child. Then he kind of felt that it interested him as a child, then I will probably like it too.
<v Etgar Keret>当他在那里时，他会想象各种各样的事情。他会想象一个纳粹不存在的世界，或者一个他不是犹太人的世界，或者一个人们总的来说不会互相残杀的世界，你知道吗？这种想法是他与童年联系在一起的。他觉得童年时这让他很感兴趣，所以我也可能会喜欢它。

00:08:56.460 --> 00:09:14.480
<v Ira Glass>Back during the war, his father couldn't leave that hole for almost two years. The Nazi headquarters was right there near the hole on the same property. A Christian farmer in his 80s would bring them food and haul out buckets of urine. They couldn't even talk a lot of the time. Etgar imagines it was like two years of meditation.
<v Ira Glass>战争期间，他父亲几乎有两年不能离开那个洞。纳粹指挥部就在洞附近的同一处房产里。一位 80 多岁的基督教农夫会给他们送食物，并把装尿的桶运出去。他们很多时候甚至无法说话。埃特加想象这就像两年的冥想。

00:09:14.480 --> 00:09:32.390
<v Etgar Keret>My father, the way that he would talk about his time in the hole, he would say that he would sleep, and then he would wake up, and then he would ask his father if the war was over. And his father would say no, so he would go and sleep some more. That's the way he would tell it to me. I think it's that kind of-- the softcore version.
<v Etgar Keret>我父亲谈论他在洞里的时光时，他会说他会睡觉，然后醒来，然后问他父亲战争是否结束了。他父亲会说没有，于是他又会继续睡。他就是这样告诉我的。我想这就是那种……轻量级版本。

00:09:32.390 --> 00:09:36.080
<v Ira Glass>And was it like, when you say a hole, could they even stand up?
<v Ira Glass>当你说是“洞”时，他们甚至能站起来吗？

00:09:36.080 --> 00:09:57.630
<v Etgar Keret>No. They couldn't stand in it, and they couldn't even lie down in it. They had to sit in it. And when the Russians liberated the town and they were brought out, they had to be carried out because their muscles were so cramped that, by the time of the end of the war, they couldn't move them.
<v Etgar Keret>不能。他们站不起来，甚至躺不下。他们必须坐在里面。当俄国人解放那个小镇，他们被带出来时，他们必须被抬出来，因为他们的肌肉在战争结束时已经蜷缩得太厉害，无法活动了。

00:09:57.630 --> 00:10:04.580
<v Ira Glass>Oh, my god. So basically they're in this hole, and they're sitting there?
<v Ira Glass>天哪。所以基本上他们一直呆在这个洞里，坐在那里？

00:10:04.580 --> 00:10:29.570
<v Etgar Keret>Yes. And you know, it's like it's very, very cold, you know? It's way beyond freezing. And you don't have food. And you hear voices in German, and you know that you can easily be killed. And you close your eyes, and you think of another universe.
<v Etgar Keret>是的。而且你知道，那里非常非常冷，你知道吗？远低于冰点。你没有食物。你听到德语的声音，你知道你很容易被杀掉。你闭上眼睛，然后想着另一个宇宙。

00:10:29.570 --> 00:10:42.460
<v Ira Glass>Etgar's written lots of fiction that imagines parallel worlds of various kinds. And when I asked him if he wanted to do something for today's show, he wrote this story-- a true story that starts when he was a kid.
<v Ira Glass>埃特加写了很多想象各种平行世界的小说。当我问他是否愿意为今天的节目做点什么时，他写了这个故事——一个从他小时候开始的真实故事。

00:10:42.460 --> 00:11:17.700
<v Etgar Keret>When I was six years old, my dad worked at a pool snack bar not far from one of Tel Aviv's beaches. Every day at 5:30 in the morning he'd leave home for the pool, swim 2 kilometers, shower in the locker room, and get to work. He wouldn't get home till 9:00 at night. 14 hours a day, 7 days a week unloading boxes of soft drinks, dressing toasted sandwiches, brewing coffee in gleaming glasses at the pool snack bar up by the beach.
<v Etgar Keret>我六岁时，我爸爸在离特拉维夫海滩不远的一个游泳池小吃吧工作。每天早上 5 点半，他就会离家去泳池，游 2 公里，在更衣室冲个澡，然后开始工作。他直到晚上 9 点才到家。每天 14 小时，每周 7 天，在海滩边的泳池小吃吧卸软饮料箱、准备吐司三明治、用闪闪发光的杯子煮咖啡。

00:11:17.700 --> 00:11:39.060
<v Etgar Keret>Years later, Dad always said those were the best days of his life, fondly remembering how the salty air blowing in from the sea would mix in his lungs with the smell of the coffee and the imported cigarettes he used to sell at the bar. Coffee, cigarettes, and the sea were always my father's three favorite things.
<v Etgar Keret>多年后，爸爸总说那是他一生中最美好的日子，深情地回忆着海风吹进肺里，混合着咖啡和他在吧台卖的进口香烟的味道。咖啡、香烟和大海，一直是我父亲最喜欢的三样东西。

00:11:39.060 --> 00:12:09.630
<v Etgar Keret>For me, those days were not quite as happy. When Dad came back from work, I was already in bed. And other than Saturdays when I'd go off to visit him at the pool with my mom, I never saw him at all. Not that I was bitter about this. Six-year-old children can't really imagine a different world for themselves, one where their father had more time for them. They accept reality as it is. And yet, I missed him dearly.
<v Etgar Keret>对我来说，那些日子并不那么快乐。当爸爸下班回来，我已经睡了。除了周六我会跟妈妈去泳池看他，我几乎见不到他。并不是我对这点感到痛苦。六岁的孩子无法真正想象一个属于他们的不同世界，一个父亲有更多时间陪伴他们的世界。他们接受现实本来如此。然而，我非常想念他。

00:12:09.630 --> 00:12:25.230
<v Etgar Keret>Seeing this, my mother suggested I go with him every morning to the pool, have a swim together, and take a cab to school from there. I don't remember anything of those morning swims, but the car rides with Dad are crystal clear.
<v Etgar Keret>看到这点，我母亲建议我每天早上和他一起去泳池，一起游泳，然后从那里坐出租车去学校。我不记得那些晨泳的任何细节，但和爸爸一起的车程却历历在目。

00:12:25.230 --> 00:13:01.190
<v Etgar Keret>It's still dark as we sit in our Silver Peugeot 504, the windows rolled down, Dad smoking Kent 100s and describing parallel universes that exist at this very moment in other dimensions-- universes where everything is precisely identical to ours-- same road, same traffic light, same cigarette in the corner of Dad's mouth-- all except for one tiny difference. And this difference Dad would change every morning, changing the one detail in the parallel universe that differed from ours.
<v Etgar Keret>天还是黑的，我们坐在银色的标致 504 里，车窗摇下，爸爸抽着健牌（Kent 100s）香烟，描述着此时此刻在其他维度中存在的平行宇宙——在那儿，一切都与我们完全相同——相同的道路、相同的交通灯、爸爸嘴角的同一支香烟——除了一个细微的差别。而这个差别，爸爸每天早上都会改变，在那个与我们不同的平行宇宙中改变这一个细节。

00:13:01.190 --> 00:13:29.110
<v Etgar Keret>Here we are in the parallel universe waiting at the exact same intersection for the light to change. Only in this universe, instead of a Silver Peugeot, we are riding a dragon. And here's another one where under my faded sweatpants hide gills which allow me to breathe under water like a fish.
<v Etgar Keret>我们正处于平行宇宙，在完全相同的十字路口等待变灯。只是在这个宇宙里，我们骑的是龙，而不是银色标致。还有另一个宇宙，在我的褪色运动裤下隐藏着鱼鳃，让我像鱼一样可以在水下呼吸。

00:13:29.110 --> 00:13:54.150
<v Etgar Keret>When I was nine, my father got a new job. He no longer had to get up so early in the morning. And in the evenings, we'd all have dinner together and watch the nightly news. I was 22 by the time I moved out of my parents' house, but I still made sure to visit them at least twice a week. And on Saturdays, I'd swim with Dad at the pool where he once worked.
<v Etgar Keret>我九岁时，父亲换了份新工作。他不再需要那么早起床了。晚上，我们全家会一起吃晚餐，看夜间新闻。我搬出父母家时已经 22 岁了，但我仍然确保每周至少去看望他们两次。周六，我会和爸爸在他曾经工作的泳池游泳。

00:13:54.150 --> 00:14:15.940
<v Etgar Keret>When I was 43, my father was diagnosed with cancer. It was tongue cancer, the result of 50 years of smoking. By the time the doctors caught on, it was at an advanced stage. And though we never spoke a word about it, it was obvious to us both that soon he was going to die.
<v Etgar Keret>我 43 岁时，父亲被诊断出患有癌症。是舌癌，吸了 50 年烟的结果。当医生发现时，已经是晚期了。虽然我们从没提起过，但我们都知道他很快就要去世了。

00:14:15.940 --> 00:14:47.220
<v Etgar Keret>On Mondays, I take him in for his physical therapy. And as we sat in the waiting room to see the physical therapist with the British accent, my father sometimes still talked about parallel universes where everything was exactly the same as ours, save for one difference-- say, that dogs could speak, or that people could read minds, or that the sky was purple. And when the milk white clouds floated across it, they looked tasty enough to eat.
<v Etgar Keret>周一，我带他去做物理治疗。当我们在候诊室等那位有英国口音的物理治疗师时，父亲偶尔还会谈论那些平行宇宙，那里的一切都和我们的一样，只是有一个不同——比如，狗会说话，或者人们会读心术，或者天空是紫色的。当乳白色的云朵飘过时，它们看起来美味得足以吞下。

00:14:47.220 --> 00:15:16.330
<v Etgar Keret>At the end of every session, the physical therapist would show me how to hold my father's arm when we walked together and what I should do in case he lost his balance. On our way home as we approached the corner of King Solomon and Arlozorov, Dad would always stop. "Do you smell that?" he'd say, and point to the new cafe at the corner. "Just by the smell of it, I can tell you that's the best coffee in town."
<v Etgar Keret>每次疗程结束时，物理治疗师都会教我如何在我们一起走路时扶着父亲的胳膊，以及万一他失去平衡我该怎么做。在回家的路上，当我们走近所罗门王和阿洛佐罗夫街的转角，爸爸总是会停下来。“你闻到了吗？”他会说，指向街角的新咖啡馆。“光凭闻味道，我就知道那是城里最好的咖啡。”

00:15:16.330 --> 00:15:51.440
<v Etgar Keret>At this stage of his illness, the tongue cancer was so advanced my father could no longer eat or drink. Instead, he got all his food and liquids through a clear plastic tube straight to his stomach. On one of these Mondays after physical therapy while walking by the corner cafe on King Solomon and Arlozorov, my father, instead of just posing to praise the place as usual, suggested we actually go in for a cup of coffee.
<v Etgar Keret>在疾病的这个阶段，舌癌已经非常严重，父亲再也无法进食或喝水了。相反，他通过一根穿过喉咙直接进入胃部的透明塑料管获取所有食物和液体。在物理治疗后的一个周一，当我们走过所罗门王和阿洛佐罗夫街的那个街角咖啡馆时，父亲没有像往常那样停下来称赞那家店，而是建议我们进去喝杯咖啡。

00:15:51.440 --> 00:16:06.700
<v Etgar Keret>"Dad," I said after a moment's hesitation, "you can't drink anything. The tumor is blocking your esophagus." "I know," he said and patted me on the back, "but you can."
<v Etgar Keret>“爸爸，”我迟疑了片刻后说，“你不能喝任何东西。肿瘤堵住了你的食道。”“我知道，”他说，拍了拍我的背，“但你可以。”

00:16:06.700 --> 00:16:36.720
<v Etgar Keret>We sat at a corner table on the sidewalk. I ordered a latte and a glass of water from the pretty waitress. And when she asked my father what he'd like, he asked for a double espresso. I stared at him, confused, and he smiled and shrugged. The waitress noticed Dad's guilty smile and gave me a questioning look. Not knowing what to say, I ordered an oatmeal cookie.
<v Etgar Keret>我们在人行道上的角落桌子坐下。我向那个漂亮的女招待点了一杯拿铁和一杯水。当她问父亲想要什么时，他点了一杯双份浓缩咖啡。我困惑地盯着他，他微笑着耸了耸肩。女招待注意到了爸爸那愧疚的微笑，给了我一个疑问的眼神。不知该说什么，我点了一块燕麦饼干。

00:16:36.720 --> 00:17:02.560
<v Etgar Keret>Until the coffee arrived, we sat in silence. I wanted to ask my father why he'd ordered the coffee at all and if it had anything to do with the waitress being pretty, but I said nothing. Dad pulled a cigarette pack out of his shirt pocket and a lighter he had stored in his glasses case and set them both down on the table. We waited.
<v Etgar Keret>在咖啡端上来之前，我们沉默地坐着。我想问父亲为什么点咖啡，是不是因为女招待很漂亮，但我没开口。爸爸从衬衫口袋里掏出一包香烟，又从眼镜盒里拿出打火机，放在桌上。我们等待着。

00:17:02.560 --> 00:17:26.530
<v Etgar Keret>A few minutes later, the waitress returned with our order. She placed it on the table-- a latte, a glass of water, and a cookie in front of me, and a double espresso in front of Dad. Aromatic steam rose from my coffee. I wanted to drink it. But doing that in Dad's face seemed unfair, so I just kept staring at it.
<v Etgar Keret>几分钟后，女招待带着我们的点单回来了。她把咖啡放在桌上——我面前是一杯拿铁、一杯水和一块饼干，爸爸面前是一杯双份浓缩咖啡。我杯里的咖啡冒着芳香的蒸汽。我很想喝。但在爸爸面前喝似乎不太公平，所以我只是盯着它。

00:17:26.530 --> 00:17:50.100
<v Etgar Keret>When out of the corner of my eye, I saw my father quickly snatch the double espresso off the table and gulp it down in one sip. It was impossible. I knew it was impossible. After all, I was there in the room when the oncologists showed me and my mother the X-ray with the tumor resting above the blocked esophagus like a scoop of vanilla ice cream on a waffle cone.
<v Etgar Keret>就在这时，我眼角余光看到父亲迅速从桌上抓起双份浓缩咖啡，一口气喝了下去。这不可能。我知道这不可能。毕竟，当肿瘤科医生带我和母亲看X光片时，我就在现场，那个肿瘤像蛋卷冰淇淋顶部的香草球一样压在堵塞的食道上方。

00:17:50.100 --> 00:18:07.920
<v Etgar Keret>My father, she said then, could never drink again. And here we were sitting together on a pleasant summer day at this hipster cafe, me still staring at my steaming cup of coffee, and him by my side, smiling after killing off his double espresso.
<v Etgar Keret>她说，我父亲再也不能喝任何东西了。然而现在，在一个惬意的夏日午后，我们坐在这家时髦的咖啡馆里，我还盯着冒着热气的咖啡，而他坐在我身边，喝掉双份浓缩咖啡后微笑着。

00:18:07.920 --> 00:18:40.800
<v Etgar Keret>And for a moment there, I thought maybe we were in a parallel universe. Maybe he told me enough stories ever since I was a little boy to tear open a hole in the aching heart of our universe through which we were sucked into a parallel one, identical to our own in every way save for one exception. In this universe, my dad could drink and eat as much as he liked, and he was not about to die in just a few months.
<v Etgar Keret>有一瞬间，我以为我们可能身处平行宇宙。也许他从我小时候起就给我讲了足够多的故事，在宇宙那令人心碎的空洞上撕开了一个口子，让我们被吸进了一个平行宇宙，那个宇宙与我们原本的宇宙在各方面都一模一样，除了一个例外。在这个宇宙里，爸爸可以随心所欲地吃喝，而且他不会在几个月内死去。

00:18:40.800 --> 00:19:08.450
<v Etgar Keret>The piping hot coffee slid down my father's windpipe and into his lungs. When it got there, Dad started to choke. He stood up and grabbed his throat with both hands. The wet wheezing noises he made were horrific-- the sound of a man whose lungs are flooded with hot coffee. A bespectacled man from the table next to us leaped up and asked my father if he needed any help.
<v Etgar Keret>滚烫的咖啡滑进了父亲的气管，进入了他的肺部。到了那里，爸爸开始窒息。他站起来，双手抓住喉咙。他发出的潮湿的喘息声太可怕了——那是一个肺部被热咖啡淹没的男人的声音。坐在我们隔壁桌的一位戴眼镜的男士跳了起来，问我父亲是否需要帮助。

00:19:08.450 --> 00:19:31.090
<v Etgar Keret>I just sat there paralyzed. The parallel universe I had shared with my father moments ago had vanished, dropping me back into a far worse universe. A few more seconds of gargling followed, after which Dad leaned over and coughed up, evacuating all the fine Italian espresso that had filled his lungs.
<v Etgar Keret>我只是呆坐在那里。刚才我和父亲共享的那个平行宇宙消失了，把我丢回了一个更糟糕的宇宙中。又持续了几秒钟的喉咙咕噜声，接着爸爸弯下腰咳嗽起来，吐出了所有填充在他肺里的意式浓缩咖啡。

00:19:31.090 --> 00:20:00.550
<v Etgar Keret>When he was done, he sat up in his chair as if nothing had happened, inches away from the puddle of coffee and phlegm, and lit himself a cigarette. People at the tables around us kept staring at him. "What did I tell you?" He smiled at me and exhaled smoke from his nostrils. "The best coffee in town."
<v Etgar Keret>咳完后，他坐在椅子上，就好像什么都没发生过一样，离那一滩咖啡和痰液只有几英寸远，然后点了一根烟。周围桌子的人一直盯着他看。“我跟你说什么来着？”他向我微笑，从鼻孔里呼出烟雾。“城里最好的咖啡。”

00:20:00.550 --> 00:20:16.650
<v Ira Glass>Etgar Keret. He's the author of many books. He writes about his dad and the rest of his family in stories a lot like this one-- emotional fables that happen to be true-- in his book, The Seven Good Years.
<v Ira Glass>埃特加·凯雷特。他是许多书的作者。他在他的书《七个好年头》（The Seven Good Years）里写了很多关于他父亲和他家人的故事，就像这个故事一样——那些恰好是真实发生的情感寓言。

00:20:16.650 --> 00:20:42.120
<v Ira Glass>Act 2, Sorry, Not Sorry. So it's kind of grim to say this, but the moon landing-- that story of American triumph-- it could've gone in another way. In some other storyline from the multiverse, Neil Armstrong and Buzz Aldrin, they land on the moon, they walk around, it's great. But then there's a problem, and they cannot take off to come home. NASA thought this was the most dangerous moment in the mission, the most likely spot it could fail.
<v Ira Glass>第二幕，《抱歉，不是真抱歉》。说这些话有点阴郁，但登月——那个关于美国胜利的故事——它本可能走向另一个结局。在多元宇宙的某个其他情节线里，尼尔·阿姆斯特朗和巴兹·奥尔德林登上了月球，四处走动，一切都很棒。但后来出了问题，他们无法起飞回家。NASA 认为这是任务中最危险的时刻，最有可能失败的地方。

00:20:42.120 --> 00:21:05.140
<v Ira Glass>One of President Richard Nixon's speechwriters, William Safire, actually prepared a statement to be read in case that was the way the story went. At the top of the page in all caps it read, "In the event of moon disaster,"-- first line of the speech-- "fate has ordained that the men who went to the moon to explore it in peace will stay on the moon to rest in peace." Pretty good.
<v Ira Glass>理查德·尼克松总统的一位演讲稿撰稿人威廉·萨菲尔（William Safire）实际上准备了一份声明，以备故事那样发展时诵读。在页面顶部全是大写字母写着：“在登月灾难发生的情况下”——演讲的第一行——“命运注定那些去月球和平探索的人将留在月球安息。”挺好的。

00:21:05.140 --> 00:21:20.150
<v Ira Glass>David Kestenbaum has this next story about another presidential speech, never delivered, that also feels like it slipped into our world from a parallel universe. You can find this speech framed, hanging in the guest bathroom of a home in California. Here's David.
<v Ira Glass>大卫·克斯滕鲍姆有下一个故事，关于另一场从未发表的总统演讲，也让人觉得它像是从平行宇宙溜进了我们的世界。你可以发现这篇演讲被装裱起来，挂在加利福尼亚州一户人家的客用浴室里。大卫，请讲。

00:21:20.150 --> 00:21:35.660
<v David Kestenbaum>The speech was to be read on the night of August 17, 1998. It's in the house of Bob Shrum, because he wrote it. Shrum was a longtime Democratic political strategist, speechwriter, and getter-outer of tight spots.
<v David Kestenbaum>这篇演讲本应在 1998 年 8 月 17 日晚上发表。它现在在鲍勃·舒姆（Bob Shrum）家里，因为是他写的。舒姆是一位资深的民主党政治策略师、演讲稿撰稿人，也是解决棘手问题的能手。

00:21:35.660 --> 00:21:38.070
<v David Kestenbaum>Are you personally good at apologizing?
<v David Kestenbaum>你个人擅长道歉吗？

00:21:38.070 --> 00:21:41.950
<v Bob Shrum>Oh, I've had to apologize so many times for so many things-- sure.
<v Bob Shrum>噢，我因为各种各样的事情道过歉太多次了——当然。

00:21:41.950 --> 00:21:45.550
<v David Kestenbaum>What's the most recent thing you apologized for?
<v David Kestenbaum>你最近一次道歉是因为什么？

00:21:45.550 --> 00:21:50.800
<v Bob Shrum>Having a fight with my wife about what we were giving various people as Christmas gifts.
<v Bob Shrum>和妻子因为我们给不同人送什么圣诞礼物而吵架。

00:21:50.800 --> 00:22:01.240
<v David Kestenbaum>This story is about an apology he wrote for a much bigger audience. That August, 1998, Shrum was on vacation in Idaho staying with some friends, and the phone rang.
<v David Kestenbaum>这个故事是关于他为更大规模的受众写的道歉词。1998 年 8 月，舒姆正在爱达荷州度假，和一些朋友在一起，电话响了。

00:22:01.240 --> 00:22:08.020
<v Bob Shrum>Well, I didn't have a cell phone then, so somehow or other-- oh, my office called me and said Mark was trying to reach me.
<v Bob Shrum>好吧，当时我没有手机，所以不知怎么的——噢，我的办公室打电话给我说马克正在试图联系我。

00:22:08.020 --> 00:22:18.160
<v David Kestenbaum>Mark is Mark Penn, who at the time was conducting polls and advising the President of the United States, who at the time was Bill Clinton, who at the time was in a lot of hot water.
<v David Kestenbaum>马克是指马克·潘（Mark Penn），他当时正在为时任美国总统比尔·克林顿进行民调并提供咨询，总统当时陷入了很大的麻烦中。

00:22:18.160 --> 00:22:27.340
<v Mark Penn>Impeachment might very well be an option-- tampering with witnesses, obstruction of justice are very, very serious charges.
<v Mark Penn>弹劾很可能是一个选择——篡改证人、妨碍司法是非常非常严重的指控。

00:22:27.340 --> 00:22:48.010
<v David Kestenbaum>A moment when one political party said there were very valid high crimes and misdemeanors, and the other party said that's ridiculous political posturing. Anyway, there had been stories in the press for months at this point that Clinton might have had an affair with an intern, Monica Lewinsky. Clinton denied it, though, under oath in a civil case, and famously on television.
<v David Kestenbaum>那是政党一方表示确实存在严重的“重罪和轻罪”，而另一方表示那是荒谬的政治作秀的时刻。不管怎样，媒体已经报道好几个月了，克林顿可能和实习生莫妮卡·莱温斯基有染。但克林顿在民事案件中宣誓作证时，以及在电视上著名地否认了这一点。

00:22:48.010 --> 00:22:48.790
<v David Kestenbaum>[CAMERAS CLICKING]
<v David Kestenbaum>[相机快门声]

00:22:48.790 --> 00:23:02.590
<v Bill Clinton>But I want to say one thing to the American people. I want you to listen to me. I'm going to say this again. I did not have sexual relations with that woman, Miss Lewinsky.
<v Bill Clinton>但我只想对美国人民说一件事。我要你们听我说。我要再说一遍。我没有和那个女人，莱温斯基小姐，发生过性关系。

00:23:02.590 --> 00:23:25.150
<v David Kestenbaum>But there was the dress with Clinton's DNA on it and recorded phone calls between Monica Lewinsky and a co-worker talking about the affair. At the time Bob Shrum got the call, President Clinton was finally going to have to testify before independent counsel Ken Starr's grand jury. He would have to own up to the affair, and then afterwards say something to the nation. But what?
<v David Kestenbaum>但那件带有克林顿 DNA 的裙子存在，还有莫妮卡·莱温斯基和一个同事谈论这段婚外情的录音电话。鲍勃·舒姆接到电话时，克林顿总统终于要到独立检察官肯·斯塔尔（Ken Starr）的大陪审团前作证了。他必须承认这段恋情，然后事后对全国说些什么。但说什么呢？

00:23:25.150 --> 00:23:54.400
<v David Kestenbaum>That was what the phone call was about. Shrum called Mark Penn back, who asked if he would take a shot at writing a speech for the president to be delivered on TV just after he gave his grand jury testimony. It would be the president's chance to frame the whole thing, maybe finally put it away and prevent the Republicans from starting impeachment proceedings. The country would be watching. Bob Shrum said OK. He hung up the phone and told his wife and the couple they were staying with on vacation what the assignment was.
<v David Kestenbaum>这就是那通电话的内容。舒姆回电给马克·潘，马克问他是否愿意试着写一篇总统演讲，在总统大陪审团作证后立即在电视上发表。这将是总统界定整个事件、也许最终平息它并阻止共和党开始弹劾程序的机会。全国都会观看。鲍勃·舒姆说好。他挂断电话，告诉了他的妻子和他们一起度假的那对夫妇这项任务。

00:23:54.400 --> 00:23:56.980
<v Bob Shrum>One of the people there said, this better be good.
<v Bob Shrum>其中一人说，这最好得写好。

00:23:56.980 --> 00:23:59.020
<v David Kestenbaum>Meaning you better do a good job?
<v David Kestenbaum>意思是说你最好表现出色？

00:23:59.020 --> 00:23:59.580
<v Bob Shrum>Yes.
<v Bob Shrum>是的。

00:23:59.580 --> 00:24:00.910
<v David Kestenbaum>Yeah.
<v David Kestenbaum>是的。

00:24:00.910 --> 00:24:11.890
<v Bob Shrum>So I sat at the dining room table and I wrote in longhand on a yellow pad, which is how I've always written. I drafted it.
<v Bob Shrum>所以我就坐在餐桌前，用黄色的记事本手写，那是我一直以来的写作方式。我起草了它。

00:24:11.890 --> 00:24:16.180
<v David Kestenbaum>What he wrote was a straight up, direct apology.
<v David Kestenbaum>他写的是一个直截了当的道歉。

00:24:16.180 --> 00:24:18.700
<v David Kestenbaum>Do you have it there with you?
<v David Kestenbaum>你带在身边了吗？

00:24:18.700 --> 00:24:22.960
<v Bob Shrum>It's on the-- well, actually, I can go get it. You want to hold on for a second?
<v Bob Shrum>它在——嗯，事实上，我可以去取。你想等一下吗？

00:24:22.960 --> 00:24:32.410
<v David Kestenbaum>Shrum went to go get it from where it was hanging in what he calls the guest powder room. And to be clear, there's other political memorabilia in there. It's a speech he's proud of.
<v David Kestenbaum>舒姆去取演讲稿了，它挂在他所谓的客用盥洗室里。要清楚，那里还有其他政治纪念品。这是他引以为豪的一篇演讲。

00:24:32.410 --> 00:24:34.530
<v David Kestenbaum>Can you read the speech for me?
<v David Kestenbaum>你能为我读一下这篇演讲吗？

00:24:34.530 --> 00:24:37.730
<v Bob Shrum>Yeah. It's not very long, so I will.
<v Bob Shrum>好的。不太长，所以我读一下。

00:24:37.730 --> 00:24:58.140
<v Bob Shrum>"My fellow Americans, no one who is not in my position can understand fully the remorse I feel today. Since I was very young, I have had a profound reverence for this office I hold. I've been honored that you, the people, have entrusted it to me. I am proud of what we have accomplished together.
<v Bob Shrum>“我的美国同胞们，没有身处我这个位置的人，无法完全理解我今天感受到的懊悔。从我很小的时候起，我就对这个职位怀有深刻的敬畏。我很荣幸你们，人民，把它托付给我。我为我们共同取得的成就感到自豪。”

00:24:58.140 --> 00:25:23.300
<v Bob Shrum>But in this case, I have fallen short of what you should expect from a president. I have failed my own religious faith and values. I have let too many people down. I take full responsibility for my actions-- for hurting my wife and daughter, for hurting Monica Lewinsky and her family, for hurting friends and staff, and for hurting the country I love. None of this ever should have happened."
<v Bob Shrum>“但在这种情况下，我没有达到你们对总统应有的期望。我辜负了自己的宗教信仰和价值观。我让太多人失望了。我为自己的行为负全责——伤害了我的妻子和女儿，伤害了莫妮卡·莱温斯基和她的家人，伤害了朋友和幕僚，伤害了我所热爱的国家。这一切都不应该发生。”

00:25:23.300 --> 00:25:28.900
<v David Kestenbaum>Then he has Clinton apologizing for misleading people about the affair. And this is how it ends.
<v David Kestenbaum>然后他让克林顿为误导人们关于这段婚外情而道歉。结尾是这样的。

00:25:28.900 --> 00:25:54.810
<v Bob Shrum>"Finally, I also want to apologize to all of you, my fellow citizens. I hope that you can find it in your heart to accept my apology. I pledge to you that I will make every effort of mind and spirit to earn your confidence again, to be worthy of this office, and to finish the work on which we have made such remarkable progress in the past six years. God bless you, and good night."
<v Bob Shrum>“最后，我也想向你们所有人，我的同胞们致歉。我希望你们能从心底里接受我的道歉。我向你们保证，我将竭尽心力重新赢得你们的信任，证明自己不负这个职位，并完成我们在过去六年里取得显著进展的工作。愿上帝保佑你们，晚安。”

00:25:54.810 --> 00:25:59.250
<v David Kestenbaum>How does it feel reading that?
<v David Kestenbaum>读这段文字感觉如何？

00:25:59.250 --> 00:26:07.670
<v Bob Shrum>I mean, I remember writing it. I thought it was the right thing for him to say. And when I hear it now, I still think it's the right thing for him to say.
<v Bob Shrum>我的意思是，我记得写它的经过。我认为这是他该说的正确的话。当我今天听到它时，我仍然认为这是他该说的正确的话。

00:26:07.670 --> 00:26:18.650
<v David Kestenbaum>It's notable that Shrum had the president apologizing to Monica Lewinsky and her family. At the time, Charles Rangel, the Democratic congressman from New York, called her, quote, "a young tramp."
<v David Kestenbaum>值得注意的是，舒姆让总统向莫妮卡·莱温斯基及其家人道歉。当时，来自纽约的民主党国会议员查尔斯·兰格尔（Charles Rangel）称她为“年轻的流浪女”。

00:26:18.650 --> 00:26:33.450
<v David Kestenbaum>She was the subject of jokes on late night television. Bill Maher said, "I think Monica Lewinsky is the one who should apologize to America." No one seemed to be thinking about her as a person whose life was now a wreck. Shrum was.
<v David Kestenbaum>她是深夜电视节目笑话的对象。比尔·马赫（Bill Maher）说：“我认为莫妮卡·莱温斯基才应该是那个向美国道歉的人。”似乎没有人把她当成一个生活已被毁掉的普通人。舒姆想到了。

00:26:33.450 --> 00:26:46.630
<v Bob Shrum>Well, I mean, she was an intern, a kid in the White House. And I thought that that made every bit of sense in the world.
<v Bob Shrum>嗯，我的意思是，她是个实习生，白宫里的一个孩子。我认为那非常有道理。

00:26:46.630 --> 00:27:02.260
<v David Kestenbaum>Shrum sent the speech into the White House. And then the day arrived. Clinton gave his grand jury testimony, which was not broadcast. And that evening, the president went on TV to give his speech. Shrum sat down with the rest of the country to watch it.
<v David Kestenbaum>舒姆把演讲稿送进了白宫。然后那一天到了。克林顿在大陪审团前作证，没有播出。那天晚上，总统上电视发表了他的演讲。舒姆和全国人民一起坐下来观看。

00:27:02.260 --> 00:27:05.470
<v Bob Shrum>We were just sitting around in the living room, watching it on television.
<v Bob Shrum>我们当时只是坐在客厅里，在电视上看着。

00:27:05.470 --> 00:27:08.830
<v David Kestenbaum>And at that point are you thinking, maybe he's going to read my speech?
<v David Kestenbaum>在那一刻，你是否在想，也许他要读我写的演讲稿了？

00:27:08.830 --> 00:27:30.740
<v Bob Shrum>Sure. I thought he might very well-- Bill Clinton, who I worked with on State of the Union messages, never exactly reads what you send him. So I didn't anticipate that it would be necessarily word for word what I had sent, but I thought it might be pretty close to it. It was almost diametrically the opposite.
<v Bob Shrum>当然。我心想他很有可能——比尔·克林顿，我和他在国情咨文中合作过，他从来不完全照读你送给他的东西。所以我没指望它一定是逐字逐句照我送的读，但我以为可能会很接近。结果完全相反。

00:27:30.740 --> 00:27:51.020
<v Bill Clinton>Good evening. This afternoon in this room, from this chair, I testified before the office of independent counsel and the grand jury. I answered their questions truthfully, including questions about my private life, questions no American citizen would ever want to answer.
<v Bill Clinton>晚上好。今天下午在这个房间里，从这把椅子上，我在独立检察官办公室和大陪审团前作证。我如实回答了他们的问题，包括关于我私生活的问题，任何美国公民都不愿回答的问题。

00:27:51.020 --> 00:28:06.020
<v Bob Shrum>I not only remember hearing the first words, but I remember the look on his face. He was really, really angry. It's the private Bill Clinton who could get angry. And I have seen him angry, but I don't think the country had seen him angry very often.
<v Bob Shrum>我不但记得听到第一句话，还记得他脸上的表情。他真的非常非常生气。那是私底下的比尔·克林顿，那个会发火的人。我见过他生气，但我认为国家还没见过他这么频繁地生气。

00:28:06.020 --> 00:28:34.640
<v Bill Clinton>As you know, in a deposition in January, I was asked questions about my relationship with Monica Lewinsky. While my answers were legally accurate, I did not volunteer information. Indeed, I did have a relationship with Miss Lewinsky that was not appropriate. In fact, it was wrong. It constituted a critical lapse in judgment and a personal failure on my part for which I am solely and completely responsible.
<v Bill Clinton>如你们所知，在 1 月的一份证词中，我被问及与莫妮卡·莱温斯基的关系。虽然我的回答在法律上是准确的，但我没有主动提供信息。事实上，我确实与莱温斯基小姐有过不恰当的关系。事实上，这是错误的。这是判断力的严重失误，是我个人的失败，我对此负全部责任。

00:28:34.640 --> 00:28:42.520
<v David Kestenbaum>There are moments of apology in there and what feel like honest reflection, like when he straight up admits the reasons he had been so evasive until then.
<v David Kestenbaum>里面有道歉的时刻，也有像诚实反思的部分，比如当他直言不讳地承认直到那时他还如此回避的原因。

00:28:42.520 --> 00:28:52.510
<v Bill Clinton>I can only tell you I was motivated by many factors-- first, by a desire to protect myself from the embarrassment of my own conduct.
<v Bill Clinton>我只能告诉你们，我受到了许多因素的驱动——首先，是为了保护自己免受自身行为尴尬的渴望。

00:28:52.510 --> 00:29:03.220
<v David Kestenbaum>But the overall message is not I'm sorry. I screwed up, he seems to be saying, but the real problem is the fact that I'm having to talk about this at all. It's a private family matter.
<v David Kestenbaum>但整体信息不是“我很抱歉”。他似乎在说，我搞砸了，但真正的问题是我居然不得不谈论这个事实。这是私人的家庭事务。

00:29:03.220 --> 00:29:10.570
<v Bill Clinton>It's nobody's business but ours. Even presidents have private lives.
<v Bill Clinton>这除了我们，不关任何人的事。即使是总统也有私生活。

00:29:10.570 --> 00:29:16.870
<v David Kestenbaum>He closes not by asking for forgiveness, as Shrum had advised, but by asking people to stop gawking.
<v David Kestenbaum>他的结尾不是像舒姆建议的那样请求宽恕，而是要求人们停止围观。

00:29:16.870 --> 00:29:44.390
<v Bill Clinton>And so tonight, I ask you to turn away from the spectacle of the past seven months, to repair the fabric of our national discourse, and to return our attention to all the challenges and all the promise of the next American century. Thank you for watching, and good night.
<v Bill Clinton>所以今晚，我要求你们不要再关注过去七个月的闹剧，修复我们国民对话的基础，并将我们的注意力转回到下一个美国世纪的所有挑战和承诺上。谢谢收看，晚安。

00:29:44.390 --> 00:29:49.190
<v David Kestenbaum>What do you think would have happened if instead that night he'd taken out your speech and read it?
<v David Kestenbaum>你认为如果那天晚上他拿出你的演讲稿读了，会发生什么？

00:29:49.190 --> 00:29:52.730
<v Bob Shrum>I think it would have lessened the chances that he would've been impeached.
<v Bob Shrum>我认为这会降低他被弹劾的机会。

00:29:52.730 --> 00:29:54.230
<v David Kestenbaum>Really?
<v David Kestenbaum>真的吗？

00:29:54.230 --> 00:29:59.890
<v Bob Shrum>I don't know that it would have prevented the impeachment, but I think it would have lessened the chances.
<v Bob Shrum>我不知道那能否阻止弹劾，但我认为那会降低概率。

00:29:59.890 --> 00:30:16.930
<v David Kestenbaum>It's possible his speech gets you to the same ending, just with a slightly different plot. And after all, there was a lot of momentum in Congress toward impeachment. But it's nice to contemplate a world where the leader of your country can stand up and acknowledge having made a mistake, can acknowledge reality.
<v David Kestenbaum>也许他的演讲能带你走到同样的结局，只是情节略有不同。毕竟，国会中弹劾的呼声很高。但考虑这样一个世界是件好事：你的国家领导人能够站出来承认犯了错误，能够承认现实。

00:30:16.930 --> 00:30:29.930
<v David Kestenbaum>An apology really is about the truth-- laying it out so we can all live in the same world together. It's impossible to imagine that happening now. Clinton seemed closer to it at that moment, but he still couldn't do it.
<v David Kestenbaum>道歉实际上是关于真相的——把它摆出来，这样我们才能共同生活在同一个世界里。很难想象现在还会发生这种事。克林顿在那一刻似乎离它更近一些，但他仍然做不到。

00:30:29.930 --> 00:30:44.080
<v David Kestenbaum>I wonder, of all the possible universes, in how many of them does Clinton say I'm sorry, you know? Not I'm sorry for what happened, or I'm sorry if people feel-- but really like I'm sorry.
<v David Kestenbaum>我很好奇，在所有可能的宇宙中，克林顿在其中多少个宇宙说了“我抱歉”？你知道吗？不是为发生了什么感到抱歉，也不是如果人们感到什么而抱歉——而是真正意义上的“我抱歉”。

00:30:44.080 --> 00:30:48.550
<v Bob Shrum>There may be a universe in which he did that. I'm just not aware of it, because I don't live in it.
<v Bob Shrum>也许有一个宇宙他那样做了。我只是不知道，因为我没生活在那里。

00:30:48.550 --> 00:30:52.010
<v David Kestenbaum>[LAUGHS] Yeah, that's not the one we live in.
<v David Kestenbaum>[笑] 是的，那不是我们生活的那个世界。

00:30:52.010 --> 00:31:07.170
<v Ira Glass>You want to know what I think? I think it's possible there is no universe in which President Clinton reads Bob Shrum's speech. Listening to the speech Clinton did give, I kind of think he gives it in every possible universe. It was exactly how he felt.
<v Ira Glass>你想知道我怎么看吗？我觉得可能根本不存在克林顿总统朗读鲍勃·舒姆演讲稿的宇宙。听着克林顿确实发表的那篇演讲，我倒觉得他在每一个可能的宇宙中都会发表那篇。那正是他的真实感受。

00:31:07.170 --> 00:31:12.360
<v David Kestenbaum>And the speech that you wrote, while great, was not at all how he was feeling at that moment.
<v David Kestenbaum>而你写的演讲稿，虽然很棒，却根本不是他当时的感觉。

00:31:12.360 --> 00:31:18.180
<v Bob Shrum>Obviously, the speech that I drafted did not reflect how he felt coming out of the grand jury.
<v Bob Shrum>显然，我草拟的演讲并没有反映他走出大陪审团时的感受。

00:31:18.180 --> 00:31:21.310
<v David Kestenbaum>In that sense, can you fault him?
<v David Kestenbaum>从这个意义上讲，你能怪他吗？

00:31:21.310 --> 00:31:41.060
<v Bob Shrum>I just think you want to do what fits the moment, and you want to be true to yourself. The problem was that, I think, he was true to the angry guy who had just come out of the room, you know? He was maybe not true to the person he was the next day.
<v Bob Shrum>我只是认为你想要做符合那一刻的事，你想忠于自己。问题是，我觉得，他忠于了那个刚走出房间的愤怒的人，你知道吗？他也许没有忠于第二天的那个人。

00:31:41.060 --> 00:32:04.080
<v David Kestenbaum>There are the yous in all the parallel worlds, but also the you on Thursday and the one on Friday, the one who's had some time to think, and some sleep, and a sandwich. For sure, those are different people.
<v David Kestenbaum>在所有平行世界里都有“你”，但也有星期四的你和星期五的那个你，那个已经有时间思考、睡了个觉、吃了顿三明治的你。显然，那些是不同的人。

00:32:04.080 --> 00:32:12.000
<v Ira Glass>David Kestenbaum. In some other universe, he stayed a physicist for all of his life. In this one, he is the executive editor of our program.
<v Ira Glass>大卫·克斯滕鲍姆。在某个其他宇宙里，他一生都是一名物理学家。在这一世，他是我们节目的执行编辑。

00:32:12.000 --> 00:32:28.620
<v Ira Glass>Bill Clinton, by the way, later wrote about this address to the nation that night in his biography. He does not mention Shrum's draft of the speech, but he does say that most of his advisors had counseled him to simply admit that he had made an awful mistake-- not to go out swinging. He did anyway, of course.
<v Ira Glass>顺便说一句，比尔·克林顿后来在他的传记中写到了那晚向全国发表的演讲。他没有提及舒姆的演讲草稿，但他确实说大多数幕僚曾建议他仅仅承认自己犯了一个可怕的错误——而不是大动干戈。当然，他还是那样做了。

00:32:28.620 --> 00:32:49.110
<v Ira Glass>Weeks after that speech, he backtracked in front of a smaller audience at a prayer breakfast with religious leaders. He took a different tone there. He said, "I agree with those who have said that in my first statement after I testified I was not contrite enough." It was much closer to what Shrum had written. He included an apology to Monica Lewinsky and her family.
<v Ira Glass>演讲几周后，他在一次宗教领袖参加的祈祷早餐会上，在较小的听众面前退了一步。他在那里采取了不同的语气。他说：“我同意那些人的说法，即我在作证后的第一次声明中不够悔恨。”那比舒姆写的内容更接近。他包括了对莫妮卡·莱温斯基及其家人的道歉。

00:32:49.110 --> 00:33:25.470
<v Ira Glass>Coming up, worried that you're living in a parallel universe where some very basic facts about your own life have been altered? That's in a minute from Chicago Public Radio when our program continues.
<v Ira Glass>接下来，担心你正生活在一个关于你自己生活的一些基本事实被篡改了的平行宇宙里？请关注芝加哥公共广播，稍后回来我们的节目继续。

00:33:25.470 --> 00:33:31.050
<v Ira Glass>We've arrived at Act 3 of our show, Act 3-- Sklar-Crossed Brothers.
<v Ira Glass>我们进入节目的第三幕，第三幕，《斯克拉家族的纠葛》。

00:33:31.050 --> 00:33:47.120
<v Ira Glass>So if you think about it, being an identical twin is kind of a parallel world situation. You know, you have this other version of yourself stuck in the same world as you, stuck in the same house as you. You see somebody who's just like you running around, doing things that you might not do.
<v Ira Glass>如果你仔细想的话，作为同卵双胞胎有点像平行世界的情况。你知道，你有另一个版本的自己被困在同一个世界里，被困在同一个房子里。你看着一个和你一模一样的人跑来跑去，做着你可能不会做的事情。

00:33:47.120 --> 00:33:53.660
<v Ira Glass>Dana Chivvis has this next story about a mother, her twins, and a 48-year-old mystery.
<v Ira Glass>达娜·奇维斯（Dana Chivvis）有下一个关于一位母亲、她的双胞胎和一个 48 年未解之谜的故事。

00:33:53.660 --> 00:34:08.020
<v Dana Chivvis>Annette Sklar had a lot going on in the winter of '72. In January, she delivered twin boys-- Jason first, followed by Randy 5 minutes later. Weighing 5 pounds, 2 ounces, Randy had to spend the first days of life in an incubator.
<v Dana Chivvis>安妮特·斯克拉（Annette Sklar）在 72 年的冬天有很多事要忙。一月份，她生下了一对双胞胎男孩——杰森先出来，兰迪 5 分钟后出来。兰迪体重 5 磅 2 盎司，不得不在保温箱里度过出生后的头几天。

00:34:08.020 --> 00:34:27.910
<v Dana Chivvis>They lived in suburban St. Louis. Annette's husband Dick had a job at a paper company, called Tension Envelope. He worked long hours and traveled a lot. Annette was often at home alone caring for two infants-- feeding them, changing diapers, and making sure she didn't lose track of which one was which, because they looked exactly alike.
<v Dana Chivvis>他们住在圣路易斯郊区。安妮特丈夫迪克（Dick）在一家名为“Tension Envelope”的造纸公司工作。他工作时间很长，经常出差。安妮特经常一个人在家照顾两个婴儿——喂奶、换尿布，并确保她不会记混哪一个是哪一个，因为他们长得一模一样。

00:34:27.910 --> 00:34:34.480
<v Dana Chivvis>Were you always scared that you might mix them up? Like from the day they were born, was that a real fear?
<v Dana Chivvis>你是否一直害怕会把他们弄混？比如从他们出生的那天起，那是一个真实的恐惧吗？

00:34:34.480 --> 00:34:52.330
<v Annette Sklar>I tried to be really careful about knowing who either had-- what outfit they were on. I knew-- I was really careful about it. So usually, we had outfits in different colors-- blue, blue Jay, and red, Randy red. And then I didn't think about it too much, once I knew it was.
<v Annette Sklar>我试着非常小心地确定是谁穿的什么衣服。我记得——我真的很小心。所以通常，我们有不同颜色的衣服——蓝色，蓝色的杰森（Jay），红色，兰迪（Randy）红。然后我就没太去想它，一旦我知道是谁。

00:34:52.330 --> 00:34:52.800
<v Dana Chivvis>OK.
<v Dana Chivvis>好的。

00:34:52.800 --> 00:34:54.820
<v Annette Sklar>[LAUGHS] That's how we did it.
<v Annette Sklar>[笑] 我们就是那样做的。

00:34:54.820 --> 00:34:56.240
<v Dana Chivvis>That's a good system.
<v Dana Chivvis>那是个好系统。

00:34:56.240 --> 00:35:09.520
<v Annette Sklar>I mean, I think I did feed them twice, one of them, and didn't feed the other one. That happens, sometimes. You did that. You did that. One of them was eating, the other one was crying a lot. And I go, why is he crying so much?
<v Annette Sklar>我的意思是，我想我确实喂过其中一个两次，而没喂另一个。这种情况有时会发生。你做过那样的事。你做过。其中一个在吃，另一个在不停地哭。我就问，为什么他哭得那么厉害？

00:35:09.520 --> 00:35:21.070
<v Dana Chivvis>She even had a back-up-- a creative redundancy she built into the safety plan for their identities. And that was diaper pins. She used blue diaper pins for Jason, and yellow ones for Randy.
<v Dana Chivvis>她甚至有一个备选方案——她在他们的身份安全计划中建立了一个创造性的冗余。那就是尿布别针。她用蓝色别针标记杰森，黄色别针标记兰迪。

00:35:21.070 --> 00:35:35.260
<v Dana Chivvis>Everything went smoothly for a while. And then at six weeks, they had their first check-up. Annette dressed them in the same outfit-- a fancy number their aunt in Los Angeles had sent them. But underneath the clothes, the diaper pin system was up and running.
<v Dana Chivvis>一切顺利了一段时间。然后在六周大的时候，他们进行了第一次检查。安妮特给他们穿上了同样的衣服——他们洛杉矶的阿姨寄给他们的一套高档货。但在衣服下面，别针系统还在运行。

00:35:35.260 --> 00:35:43.690
<v Dana Chivvis>At the doctor's office, a nurse took the babies out of the room. Annette can't really remember why. Maybe she needed to weigh them. And a few minutes later, the nurse came back in with the boys.
<v Dana Chivvis>在医生办公室，护士把婴儿带出了房间。安妮特记不清原因了。也许她需要称重。几分钟后，护士带着男孩们回来了。

00:35:43.690 --> 00:35:53.040
<v Annette Sklar>And she goes, oh, we have such a great surprise for you. Now we have Pampers. We're just using them for the first time. So instead of using your diapers, we used your Pampers.
<v Annette Sklar>她说，噢，我们有个大惊喜给你。我们现在有“帮宝适”（Pampers）。我们第一次用它们。所以代替用你们的尿布，我们用了你们的“帮宝适”。

00:35:53.040 --> 00:35:53.920
<v Dana Chivvis>Oh, no.
<v Dana Chivvis>噢，不。

00:35:53.920 --> 00:35:58.270
<v Annette Sklar>And then they gave me all four of their tins.
<v Annette Sklar>然后他们把四个别针都还给了我。

00:35:58.270 --> 00:36:10.450
<v Dana Chivvis>The nurse had changed the babies out of their cloth diapers and put them into disposable ones, foiling the system entirely. She handed Annette a little bag with the diaper pins in it. The boys were completely indistinguishable.
<v Dana Chivvis>护士把婴儿的布尿布换成了纸尿裤，完全破坏了那个系统。她递给安妮特一个小袋子，里面装着尿布别针。男孩们变得完全无法区分了。

00:36:10.450 --> 00:36:19.270
<v Annette Sklar>I was very upset. So they go, oh, you're going to be fine. You're fine. Of course, they go, you're fine. And we got in the car, and I was so upset the whole way home.
<v Annette Sklar>我非常沮丧。他们说，噢，你会没事的。你会没事的。当然，他们会说，你会没事的。我们上了车，我一路上都非常难过。

00:36:19.270 --> 00:36:30.370
<v Dana Chivvis>When she got home, Annette put the babies on the couch and stared at them. At six weeks old, they didn't have distinct personalities yet. They weighed the same amount. They had the same bald heads. No birthmarks.
<v Dana Chivvis>到家后，安妮特把婴儿放在沙发上盯着他们看。六周大的时候，他们还没有个性。他们的体重一样。头都是秃的。没有胎记。

00:36:30.370 --> 00:36:36.420
<v Annette Sklar>And I'm not kidding you, they looked exactly alike. And so I was thinking I was a really terrible mother.
<v Annette Sklar>我不骗你，他们长得一模一样。所以我当时想，我真的是个糟糕的母亲。

00:36:36.420 --> 00:36:36.700
<v Dana Chivvis>Oh, no.
<v Dana Chivvis>噢，不。

00:36:36.700 --> 00:36:42.940
<v Annette Sklar>Then how can I really look at this and not see my own children and not know which ones they are?
<v Annette Sklar>那怎么能让我看着这却看不出自己的孩子，也不知道他们是谁？

00:36:42.940 --> 00:36:55.600
<v Linda Wallace>You know, she called me in a panic. And she just said, I don't know what I'm going to do. I don't know which one's which. I don't know what to do.
<v Linda Wallace>你知道，她惊慌失措地给我打了电话。她只是说，我不知道该怎么办。我不知道哪一个是哪个。我不知道该做什么。

00:36:55.600 --> 00:37:02.470
<v Dana Chivvis>This is Annette's best friend Linda Wallace-- Aunt Linda, as Jason and Randy know her. She lived in the same subdivision.
<v Dana Chivvis>这是安妮特最好的朋友琳达·华莱士（Linda Wallace）——也就是杰森和兰迪所称的琳达阿姨。她住在同一个小区。

00:37:02.470 --> 00:37:04.360
<v Dana Chivvis>Did you go over there to try to help her?
<v Dana Chivvis>你过去试着帮她了吗？

00:37:04.360 --> 00:37:05.030
<v Linda Wallace>No, I didn't.
<v Linda Wallace>没有，我没有。

00:37:05.030 --> 00:37:05.330
<v Dana Chivvis>Yeah.
<v Dana Chivvis>是的。

00:37:05.330 --> 00:37:10.690
<v Linda Wallace>Because if she couldn't tell them apart, how would I be able to tell them apart?
<v Linda Wallace>因为如果连她都分不清，我怎么能分清呢？

00:37:10.690 --> 00:37:11.500
<v Dana Chivvis>Right.
<v Dana Chivvis>对。

00:37:11.500 --> 00:37:18.600
<v Linda Wallace>I mean, I couldn't tell them apart, you know? She had the pins.
<v Linda Wallace>我的意思是，我分不清他们，你知道吗？她有别针。

00:37:18.600 --> 00:37:21.100
<v Dana Chivvis>Yeah. [LAUGHS]
<v Dana Chivvis>是的。[笑]

00:37:21.100 --> 00:37:24.260
<v Dana Chivvis>Were you nervous to tell Dick? Were you nervous to tell your husband?
<v Dana Chivvis>你告诉迪克时紧张吗？你告诉丈夫时紧张吗？

00:37:24.260 --> 00:37:24.570
<v Annette Sklar>Yes.
<v Annette Sklar>是的。

00:37:24.570 --> 00:37:25.000
<v Dana Chivvis>You were?
<v Dana Chivvis>你紧张？

00:37:25.000 --> 00:37:40.750
<v Annette Sklar>Yes. I was really nervous. So when he came home, and he saw them, and they were in their crib-- and so finally, I had to tell him. I go, I have to tell you what happened. And I told him. And he goes, really? He goes, oh, we'll figure it out. Let's see.
<v Annette Sklar>是的。我真的很紧张。所以当他回家看到他们，他们都在婴儿床上——最后，我不得不告诉他。我说，我得告诉你发生了什么。我告诉了他。他说，真的？他说，噢，我们会弄明白的。看看。

00:37:40.750 --> 00:37:59.780
<v Annette Sklar>And then we were both looking at both of them right next to each other. And then of course, they had the Pampers on, and nothing. And he's going, I don't know. What do you think it is? (LAUGHING) I go, I don't know.
<v Annette Sklar>然后我们俩都盯着靠在一起的他们。当然，他们穿着“帮宝适”，什么也没有。他问，我不知道。你觉得是谁？（笑）我说，我不知道。

00:37:59.780 --> 00:38:11.720
<v Dana Chivvis>Annette was faced with a decision. She had to choose which baby was Jason and which one was Randy. She had nothing to go on but a maternal feeling, so she chose. Dick agreed with her.
<v Dana Chivvis>安妮特面临一个决定。她必须选择哪一个是杰森，哪一个是兰迪。她没有任何依据，只有一种母亲的感觉，所以她选了。迪克同意了她的说法。

00:38:11.720 --> 00:38:25.150
<v Annette Sklar>I really, always felt like I had the right person in my heart. I just said it is, and I'm not going to think about it anymore. This is my life. We know who they are, and it is who they are. And we're not going to talk about it anymore.
<v Annette Sklar>我真的，一直觉得我的心里有正确的人选。我只是说就是他，我不会再想了。这就是我的生活。我们知道他们是谁，就是他们是谁。我们不会再谈论它了。

00:38:25.150 --> 00:38:27.890
<v Dana Chivvis>Aunt Linda? Not so convinced.
<v Dana Chivvis>琳达阿姨？不那么确信。

00:38:27.890 --> 00:38:30.840
<v Dana Chivvis>Do you think she got it right? Do you think Annette got it right?
<v Dana Chivvis>你觉得她选对了吗？你觉得安妮特选对了吗？

00:38:30.840 --> 00:38:31.670
<v Linda Wallace>I don't know.
<v Linda Wallace>我不知道。

00:38:31.670 --> 00:38:31.860
<v Dana Chivvis>You don't know?
<v Dana Chivvis>你不知道？

00:38:31.860 --> 00:38:50.020
<v Linda Wallace>I mean, I don't think she knows. She says she did, but how does she know? I mean, there's no way. They were so-- they looked so much alike, and so I'm not sure.
<v Linda Wallace>我的意思是，我觉得她不知道。她说她做到了，但她怎么知道？我的意思是，没可能。他们太——他们长得太像了，所以我不太确定。

00:38:50.020 --> 00:39:01.660
<v Dana Chivvis>Jason and Randy are 48 years old now. They're comedians. They work together. They've done cameos on different TV shows, like Curb Your Enthusiasm and Better Call Saul. And they still look exactly the same.
<v Dana Chivvis>杰森和兰迪现在 48 岁了。他们是喜剧演员。他们在一起工作。他们曾在不同的电视剧里客串，比如《抑制热情》（Curb Your Enthusiasm）和《风骚律师》（Better Call Saul）。他们现在看起来还是一模一样。

00:39:01.660 --> 00:39:05.230
<v Dana Chivvis>Randy, can you describe what Jason looks like?
<v Dana Chivvis>兰迪，你能描述一下杰森长什么样吗？

00:39:05.230 --> 00:39:18.910
<v Randy Sklar>He's very good looking. Jason, he is very Semitic looking. He's got glasses. He's about 5 foot, 8 and 1/2.
<v Randy Sklar>他长得很好看。杰森，他看起来很像闪族人（Semitic）。他戴眼镜。身高大约 5 英尺 8 英寸半。

00:39:18.910 --> 00:39:20.290
<v Jason Sklar>That's generous.
<v Jason Sklar>够大方的。

00:39:20.290 --> 00:39:22.960
<v Randy Sklar>Somewhat athletic looking.
<v Randy Sklar>看起来有点运动感。

00:39:22.960 --> 00:39:24.460
<v Jason Sklar>Less generous.
<v Jason Sklar>没那么大方。

00:39:24.460 --> 00:39:48.650
<v Dana Chivvis>They found out about their potentially swapped identities when they were about 12 years old, by accident. They were spending a summer weekend at the world famous Lake of the Ozarks in central Missouri with their mom and Aunt Linda and her two sons. The boys were playing while Annette and Aunt Linda talked amongst themselves. The story of the calamitous doctor's visit came up. And unbeknownst to them, Jason and Randy were in earshot, and they overheard the whole thing.
<v Dana Chivvis>他们大约 12 岁时偶然发现了他们身份可能互换的事。他们和妈妈、琳达阿姨以及她的两个儿子在密苏里州中部的世界著名景点奥扎克湖度周末。孩子们在玩耍时，安妮特和琳达阿姨私下谈话。那个灾难性的医生之行的话题被提了起来。在他们不知情的情况下，杰森和兰迪在旁边听到了全程。

00:39:48.650 --> 00:39:52.030
<v Jason Sklar>And we were just-- we hated the story.
<v Jason Sklar>我们当时简直——我们讨厌那个故事。

00:39:52.030 --> 00:39:55.410
<v Dana Chivvis>This is Jason. He sounds remarkably like Randy.
<v Dana Chivvis>这是杰森。他的声音和兰迪惊人地像。

00:39:55.410 --> 00:40:15.670
<v Jason Sklar>We were livid that this could have happened, and also that she was sharing it in sort of an offhand kind of joking way. And Linda Wallace, who is a funny woman, was making it funnier. And they were laughing about it. And we were like-- we were just very embarrassed by it and upset by it. Like, oh my god, this is an insane thing to hear, because we may not be who we are.
<v Jason Sklar>我们对这种事竟然会发生感到愤怒，也对她以一种随意的开玩笑方式分享它感到生气。琳达·华莱士，一个风趣的女人，把故事讲得更有趣了。她们在笑。我们当时觉得——我们为它感到非常尴尬和难过。就像，天哪，听到这个太疯狂了，因为我们可能根本不是我们自己。

00:40:15.670 --> 00:40:19.390
<v Dana Chivvis>What pissed you off about it? What was so upsetting about it?
<v Dana Chivvis>什么事让你感到愤怒？什么事让你那么难过？

00:40:19.390 --> 00:40:38.340
<v Jason Sklar>I think that it just was we were just sensitive, I think, to being confused. And again, it was this narrative that kind of went through our lives, as we wanted to be individuals and we wanted to be seen as individuals. And then here's this thing that happened where we could be so easily confused.
<v Jason Sklar>我想我们只是很敏感，我觉得，对被搞混很敏感。同样，它是那种贯穿我们生活的叙事，我们想成为独立个体，希望被视为独立个体。然后发生了这件事，我们竟然可以这么轻易地被混淆。

00:40:38.340 --> 00:40:49.720
<v Dana Chivvis>For identical twins-- two people who are constantly being mistaken for one another, who try really hard to make it clear that they're unique individuals-- this was a big deal. Randy was the most upset.
<v Dana Chivvis>对于同卵双胞胎——这两个人经常被认错，并且努力去明确他们是独特的个体——这是一件大事。兰迪是最难过的。

00:40:49.720 --> 00:40:57.610
<v Annette Sklar>He goes, why can't you go to the policemen? I think Randy really wanted me to go there. We were talking all the way to the car.
<v Annette Sklar>他说，为什么不能去找警察？我想兰迪真的希望我去。我们在去车的路上一直在谈论这个。

00:40:57.610 --> 00:41:13.610
<v Dana Chivvis>Not that an actual crime had been committed, just a crime against their dignity. But for some reason, Randy figured the police were the ones who could sort it out. But Annette did not go to the police. And the name confusion did not get sorted out, it remained a mystery in Jason and Randy's lives.
<v Dana Chivvis>并不是说真的发生了什么罪行，只是对他们尊严的一种冒犯。但不知何故，兰迪认为警察是可以解决问题的人。但安妮特没有报警。名字的混淆并没有被解决，它成了杰森和兰迪生活中的一个谜。

00:41:13.610 --> 00:41:25.320
<v Linda Wallace>The couple times that it was brought up throughout their years, if somebody said something about it, they would go, I don't want to hear about it. Don't bring it up again.
<v Linda Wallace>多年来只要提到过几次，如果有人提起它，他们就会说，我不想听。别再提了。

00:41:25.320 --> 00:41:31.080
<v Dana Chivvis>Wow. I had no idea it was that painful for them.
<v Dana Chivvis>哇。我不知道这对他们来说那么痛苦。

00:41:31.080 --> 00:41:42.860
<v Linda Wallace>I mean, it was a joke between Annette and I that I'm not sure was a joke between all of us, because they were very sensitive about it.
<v Linda Wallace>我的意思是，那曾是安妮特和我之间的一个笑话，但我不敢说那是我们所有人的笑话，因为他们对此非常敏感。

00:41:42.860 --> 00:41:53.030
<v Randy Sklar>Just the simple fact of the matter that for countless times in our lives, someone has said to me, Jason? And I'm like, nope, I'm Randy.
<v Randy Sklar>就是生活中有无数次，有人对我说，“杰森？”而我会说，“不，我是兰迪。”

00:41:53.030 --> 00:41:53.390
<v Dana Chivvis>Right.
<v Dana Chivvis>对。

00:41:53.390 --> 00:41:59.510
<v Randy Sklar>You know? Like, that that's happened so many times in my life. If that's not true, there is--
<v Randy Sklar>你知道吗？那种事在我生命中发生了太多次。如果这不是真的，这里有——

00:41:59.510 --> 00:42:01.550
<v Jason Sklar>You have to go back to every single one of those people and apologize.
<v Jason Sklar>你必须回去向所有那些人道歉。

00:42:01.550 --> 00:42:09.920
<v Randy Sklar>--and apologize. No, but there's a notion that you've been living a little bit of a lie. It's a tiny lie, but it's not your lie.
<v Randy Sklar>——去道歉。不，但有一种观念是，你一直在过着一种谎言。这是一个微小的谎言，但它不是你的谎言。

00:42:09.920 --> 00:42:10.610
<v Jason Sklar>Not your lie.
<v Jason Sklar>不是你的谎言。

00:42:10.610 --> 00:42:20.890
<v Randy Sklar>It's not your lie, and it's not your fault. But still, that's unsettling in a weird way, to me. That's just weird that for all this time I had it wrong.
<v Randy Sklar>它不是你的谎言，也不是你的错。但即便如此，那依然让我感到不安，以一种奇怪的方式。这只是很奇怪，因为这么长时间以来我一直都错了。

00:42:20.890 --> 00:42:48.330
<v Dana Chivvis>If they were switched, then they have to re-imagine something that's so foundational nobody ever thinks about it-- their birth. Who's older? Who was in the incubator? Like Randy says, it's weird. It doesn't really make a difference, but also somehow it does. And not knowing what the truth is, that's even more unnerving.
<v Dana Chivvis>如果他们确实被换了，那么他们必须重新想象一些非常基础的东西，没人会想到它——他们的出生。谁大？谁在保温箱里？就像兰迪说的，这很奇怪。它并没有真正带来什么不同，但以某种方式它又很重要。而不知道真相是什么，这更让人心烦意乱。

00:42:48.330 --> 00:43:11.370
<v Dana Chivvis>It turns out there was one way to figure it out. Sometime around the age of 40-- Randy can't remember exactly when-- the topic of their potentially swapped identities came up. And they were like, why haven't we figured this out yet? They were grown ass adults. They should know who they actually were. And also, they were working as comedians at that point, and they thought it might be good material, which-- we'll see.
<v Dana Chivvis>事实证明，有一个办法可以弄清楚。大约在 40 岁的时候——兰迪不记得具体是什么时候——他们潜在的身份互换的话题又被提出来了。他们想，为什么我们还没弄清楚这个？他们是成年人了。他们应该知道他们实际上是谁。而且，当时他们正以喜剧演员为生，他们认为这可能是很好的素材，嗯——我们拭目以待。

00:43:11.370 --> 00:43:28.410
<v Dana Chivvis>Randy thought maybe the hospital where they were born would have a copy of their baby fingerprints. They could get those and compare them to their adult fingerprints. He wasn't wrong about the science part of that. Identical twins have nearly the same DNA, so their fingerprint patterns are very similar-- closer than anyone else's.
<v Dana Chivvis>兰迪想，也许他们出生的医院会有婴儿指纹的副本。他们可以拿到那些指纹，并把它们与成人的指纹进行比较。他关于科学部分的推断没错。同卵双胞胎几乎有相同的 DNA，所以他们的指纹图案非常相似——比任何其他人都更接近。

00:43:28.410 --> 00:43:49.760
<v Dana Chivvis>As you probably know, fingerprints are made up of lines. And some of those lines have tiny variations in them, like forks or loops. And those markings are different between identical twins. For those of you still listening, the reason for that is that the markings develop based on the conditions in the womb, like pressure, which is different depending on where the baby is in the womb.
<v Dana Chivvis>正如你可能知道的那样，指纹由线条组成。其中一些线条有微小的变化，比如叉状或环状。这些标记在同卵双胞胎之间是不同的。对于还在听的各位，原因是这些标记的发展基于子宫内的状况，比如压力，这取决于婴儿在子宫里的位置而有所不同。

00:43:49.760 --> 00:44:00.800
<v Dana Chivvis>So Randy called the hospital where they were born to see if they still had a copy of their fingerprints. The hospital told them that they throw birth records out after 30 years. They were old and out of luck.
<v Dana Chivvis>所以兰迪给他们出生的医院打了电话，看他们是否还有指纹的副本。医院告诉他们，出生记录超过 30 年就会被丢弃。他们年龄大了，没指望了。

00:44:00.800 --> 00:44:04.620
<v Randy Sklar>Until I was back home last summer with my family--
<v Randy Sklar>直到去年夏天我带家人回家——

00:44:04.620 --> 00:44:05.750
<v Dana Chivvis>This is Randy.
<v Dana Chivvis>这是兰迪。

