<v Linda Wallace>我也爱你。

00:52:49.630 --> 00:53:13.520
<v Dana Chivvis>So all is well in the Sklar family. Jason is still older than Randy by five minutes. Randy is still the one who spent time in an incubator. They haven't been committing unintentional fraud. They don't have to change their social security numbers. The world Annette said they were living in was indeed the world they were in all along.
<v Dana Chivvis>所以斯克拉家族一切安好。杰森仍然比兰迪大五分钟。兰迪依然是那个在保温箱里度过时光的人。他们没有进行无意的欺诈。他们不必更改社会安全号码。安妮特所说的他们一直生活在其中的世界，确实是他们一直生活在其中的世界。

00:53:13.520 --> 00:53:19.560
<v Ira Glass>Dana Chivvis is one of the producers of our show.
<v Ira Glass>达娜·奇维斯是我们节目的制作人之一。

00:53:19.560 --> 00:53:32.030
<v Ira Glass>Act 4, If I Lived Here, I'd Be Home Now. So our last story today is about somebody who tries to visit a parallel world, one that she almost lived in herself. Diane Wu explains.
<v Ira Glass>第四幕，《如果我住在这里，我现在就到家了》。今天我们的最后一个故事是关于一个试图访问一个平行世界的人，一个她差点就生活在其中的世界。黛安·吴（Diane Wu）来解释。

00:53:32.030 --> 00:53:43.350
<v Diane Wu>The universe Emily ended up in was like a postcard for old-timey America-- 10 acres in Vermont with five big brothers and sisters eating candy out of glass jars at the family general store.
<v Diane Wu>艾米丽（Emily）最终进入的那个宇宙就像一张老式美国的明信片——在佛蒙特州有 10 英亩地，五个哥哥姐姐在家庭杂货店吃着玻璃罐里的糖果。

00:53:43.350 --> 00:53:53.100
<v Emily>Yeah, we just lived in this big house. And my parents were really strict. [LAUGHS] My parents were so strict. They always reminded me of the Von Trapp family, even though that's really extreme.
<v Emily>是的，我们住在这一所大房子里。我父母真的很严厉。[笑] 我父母太严厉了。他们总是让我想到冯·特拉普（Von Trapp）家族，尽管那有点太极端。

00:53:53.100 --> 00:53:55.840
<v Diane Wu>Mm-hmm. A lot of rules, huh?
<v Diane Wu>嗯。很多规矩，对吧？

00:53:55.840 --> 00:53:58.860
<v Emily>It was a lot of rules. It was very much do as I say.
<v Emily>有很多规矩。基本上就是“照我说的做”。

00:53:58.860 --> 00:54:20.370
<v Diane Wu>She almost lived a totally different life. Emily was adopted from South Korea when she was a baby. She's 40 now. And as a kid, she rarely thought about the world she came from, what her life would have been like there. It was the '80s. That kind of thing wasn't especially encouraged. Her family raised her not to ask many questions about her past, but there was one thing she really wanted to know about it.
<v Diane Wu>她差点过上完全不同的生活。艾米丽是婴儿时被从韩国领养的。她现在 40 岁。小时候，她很少思考她来自的世界，在那里的生活会是什么样子。那是 80 年代。那种事情并不特别受鼓励。她的家人抚养她时尽量不去问关于她过去的太多问题，但有一件事她真的很想知道。

00:54:20.370 --> 00:54:24.770
<v Emily>I mean, mostly growing up I just really wondered what my birth mother looked like.
<v Emily>我的意思是，成长过程中我大多只是真的很想知道我的亲生母亲长什么样。

00:54:24.770 --> 00:54:26.610
<v Diane Wu>Mm-hmm.
<v Diane Wu>嗯。

00:54:26.610 --> 00:54:30.540
<v Emily>That was usually the question that always came to my mind.
<v Emily>那通常是我脑子里一直出现的问题。

00:54:30.540 --> 00:54:42.090
<v Diane Wu>Her adoptive parents didn't look like her. They were white, as was most everybody else in town, so it was hard for her to picture her birth mother's face. But there was no one to ask.
<v Diane Wu>她的领养父母长得不像她。他们是白人，就像镇上几乎所有人一样，所以她很难想象亲生母亲的脸。但没人可以问。

00:54:42.090 --> 00:54:55.470
<v Diane Wu>A couple of decades later, Emily became a mother herself and suddenly, felt connected to and curious about her birth mother again. She started researching how to contact her and, two long years later, finally got in touch.
<v Diane Wu>几十年后，艾米丽自己也成了母亲，突然又感觉到与亲生母亲的联系和好奇。她开始研究如何联系她，两年后，终于取得了联系。

00:54:55.470 --> 00:55:17.070
<v Diane Wu>Then she decided to go on a trip to South Korea to meet her birth mother-- a trip that would allow her to tunnel between the universe that was into the universe that might have been. She recorded parts of it for a podcast called, Motherhood Sessions. All of Emily's emotions about the trip hit her on the way to the airport in New York when suddenly she found herself sobbing while talking to the cab driver.
<v Diane Wu>然后她决定去韩国见她的亲生母亲——一趟能让她在已有的宇宙和可能存在的宇宙之间穿梭的旅行。她为播客《母亲会议》（Motherhood Sessions）录制了部分片段。艾米丽对这次旅行的所有情绪在去纽约机场的路上爆发了，当时她突然在与出租车司机交谈时哭了起来。

00:55:17.070 --> 00:55:19.830
<v Cab Driver>Do you have any family in Korea?
<v Cab Driver>你在韩国有家人吗？

00:55:19.830 --> 00:55:23.060
<v Emily>I'm meeting my birth mother for the first time.
<v Emily>我要第一次见我的亲生母亲。

00:55:23.060 --> 00:55:28.270
<v Cab Driver>That is exciting for you. Who told about your birth mother?
<v Cab Driver>这对你来说很令人兴奋。谁告诉你的亲生母亲？

00:55:28.270 --> 00:55:36.820
<v Emily>I searched for her through the adoption agency. It took two years-- two years of searching.
<v Emily>我通过领养机构找到了她。花了两年时间——两年的寻找。

00:55:36.820 --> 00:55:39.940
<v Cab Driver>Oh, that's good. That's good. And what about your dad?
<v Cab Driver>噢，那很好。那很好。那你的父亲呢？

00:55:39.940 --> 00:55:40.800
<v Emily>I don't know.
<v Emily>我不知道。

00:55:40.800 --> 00:55:41.310
<v Cab Driver>You don't know?
<v Cab Driver>你不知道？

00:55:41.310 --> 00:55:42.230
<v Emily>I don't know.
<v Emily>我不知道。

00:55:42.230 --> 00:56:01.590
<v Cab Driver>OK, don't worry about that anything. It should be better, everything. And it's a very blessing for you. You're going to meet your mother. I know. I know you're feeling, because you've spend your whole life without your mom.
<v Cab Driver>没关系，别担心。一切都会好起来的。这对你来说是一种巨大的祝福。你要去见你的母亲。我知道。我知道你的感受，因为你一生都在没有妈妈的情况下度过。

00:56:01.590 --> 00:56:06.490
<v Emily>[SOBS]
<v Emily>[抽泣]

00:56:06.490 --> 00:56:14.620
<v Cab Driver>I know you spent your whole life without your mom. You need your mom on when your children-- like you babies, you know?
<v Cab Driver>我知道你一生都没有妈妈。当你有孩子的时候你真的需要妈妈——就像你的孩子那样，你知道吗？

00:56:14.620 --> 00:56:17.080
<v Emily>Yeah.
<v Emily>是的。

00:56:17.080 --> 00:56:34.250
<v Emily>Going on the plane, too, for me, it was kind of thinking about the last time I took that flight was when I was a baby. And touching down, I remember thinking that the last time I was here was when I was five months old.
<v Emily>上飞机对我来说，也是在想最后一次坐那次航班是我婴儿的时候。落地时，我记得想到我最后一次来这里是我五个月大的时候。

00:56:34.250 --> 00:56:45.760
<v Diane Wu>She had a day to settle in before she met her birth mother. It was the first time she'd ever been to Asia. And wandering around Seoul, it was so easy to imagine the other versions of her life all around her.
<v Diane Wu>在见亲生母亲之前，她有一天的调整时间。这是她第一次去亚洲。漫步在首尔，想象她周围各种版本的自己生活太容易了。

00:56:45.760 --> 00:57:13.900
<v Emily>I'd go in stores, or in restaurants, and I'd think, oh, could I be that woman behind the counter working there? When I'd see a mom with her little girl, I'd be like, oh, wow, is that what it would've been like to walk down the street holding my mom's hand?
<v Emily>我走进商店或餐馆，会想，噢，我能不能是那个在柜台工作的女人？当我看到一个带着小女孩的妈妈，我会想，噢，哇，如果拉着我妈的手在街上走会是什么样？

00:57:13.900 --> 00:57:27.130
<v Diane Wu>On her second day in Korea, Emily carefully did her hair and makeup and headed to the adoption agency to meet her birth mother. Their reunion happened in a worn down meeting room in front of a bulletin board pinned with Christmas cards.
<v Diane Wu>她在韩国的第二天，艾米丽仔细梳妆打扮，前往领养机构见她的亲生母亲。她们的重逢发生在一个破旧的会议室里，前面是一块贴着圣诞卡的公告板。

00:57:27.130 --> 00:57:45.680
<v Emily>I see this short Korean woman walk in the door. And we hug, and I cry a lot.
<v Emily>我看到这个矮小的韩国女人走进门。我们拥抱，我大哭了一场。

00:57:45.680 --> 00:57:47.160
<v Emily's Birth Mother>[SPEAKING KOREAN]
<v Emily's Birth Mother>[说韩语]

00:57:47.160 --> 00:57:51.930
<v Emily>And she says [KOREAN], which means I'm sorry.
<v Emily>她说“（韩语）”，意思是“我很抱歉”。

00:57:51.930 --> 00:57:54.520
<v Diane Wu>It's the very first thing her birth mother says to her.
<v Diane Wu>这是她亲生母亲对她说的第一句话。

00:57:54.520 --> 00:58:03.950
<v Emily>[SPEAKING KOREAN]. And I say [KOREAN], which means it's OK. [KOREAN]
<v Emily>（韩语）。我说“（韩语）”，意思是“没关系”。（韩语）

00:58:03.950 --> 00:58:09.390
<v Emily>[LAUGHTER]
<v Emily>[笑声]

00:58:09.390 --> 00:58:11.020
<v Emily>It's like looking in a mirror.
<v Emily>这就像照镜子一样。

00:58:11.020 --> 00:58:12.870
<v Social Worker>[SPEAKING KOREAN]
<v Social Worker>[说韩语]

00:58:12.870 --> 00:58:27.300
<v Diane Wu>That's a social worker in the room translating for them. Emily's birth mother told her her story. She'd gotten pregnant as a teenager, which was completely shunned. There was no place in society for teenage single mothers.
<v Diane Wu>那是房间里的社工为她们翻译。艾米丽的亲生母亲告诉了她她的故事。她十几岁时怀孕，这受到了彻底的蔑视。社会上没有未婚未成年母亲的一席之地。

00:58:27.300 --> 00:58:43.560
<v Diane Wu>A few days after Emily was born, a relative came by with a police officer and social worker, and they took Emily away. A week later, Emily's birth mother went to the police station, desperate to get her baby back. They told her it was too late.
<v Diane Wu>艾米丽出生几天后，一位亲戚带着警察和社工来了，他们带走了艾米丽。一周后，艾米丽的亲生母亲赶到警察局，渴望要回她的孩子。他们告诉她太迟了。

00:58:43.560 --> 00:59:09.960
<v Diane Wu>She called the adoption agency several times over the years to check in on her daughter. Learned she'd been adopted by an American family. I talked with her on the phone. And when I asked her if she'd ever imagine the world where she did manage to get Emily back, she told me, I haven't really had those thoughts, but I always dreamt about her.
<v Diane Wu>多年来她多次打电话给领养机构询问女儿的情况。得知她被一个美国家庭领养了。我和她在电话里聊过。当我问她是否想象过她成功要回艾米丽的世界时，她告诉我，我没有真正那样想过，但我一直梦到她。

00:59:09.960 --> 00:59:25.860
<v Diane Wu>Seeing her birth mother's face, meeting her whole extended family later on that trip, it had a profound impact on Emily. I heard her talk about it on that podcast, Motherhood Sessions. And I was surprised and moved to hear her say that being in the same room with her birth family--
<v Diane Wu>看到亲生母亲的脸，在那次旅行后期见到她所有的大家族，对艾米丽产生了深远的影响。我在那个播客《母亲会议》里听到她谈论这件事。当她听到她身处亲生家庭的同一个房间时，我感到惊讶和感动——

00:59:25.860 --> 00:59:59.920
<v Emily>For the first time, I felt pretty, you know? Because growing up, your parents always tell you that you're pretty, or whatever. But I never really believed it. But being around this family, and we all look alike, you know? I felt like I was beautiful. And I had never felt it that deeply before.
<v Emily>这是我第一次觉得我很漂亮，你知道吗？因为成长过程中，你的父母总是告诉你你很漂亮，无论如何。但我从来没真正相信过。但和这个家庭在一起，我们看起来都一样，你知道吗？我觉得我是美丽的。我以前从未如此深刻地感觉到它。

00:59:59.920 --> 01:00:04.220
<v Diane Wu>And what do you think is behind that feeling?
<v Diane Wu>你认为这种感觉背后的原因是什么？

01:00:04.220 --> 01:00:32.150
<v Emily>Because after growing up, living a lifetime of feeling like you don't fit in and wondering why maybe certain guys don't like you, or why maybe you're not the most popular person in school. You know, for the first time in my life, I felt like I made sense. I felt like my being made sense. I felt like my face made sense.
<v Emily>因为成长过程中，生活在一种觉得自己不合群的感觉里，想知道为什么有些男生不喜欢你，或者为什么你可能不是学校里最受欢迎的人。你知道，这是我人生中第一次，我觉得我有了意义。我觉得我的存在有了意义。我觉得我的脸有了意义。

01:00:32.150 --> 01:00:52.580
<v Diane Wu>Emily stayed with her birth family for three days in a town called Dongducheon in a little house by the railroad tracks. There were ceramic pots of kimchi in the yard, a flat screen TV in the living room that was always playing Korean variety shows and soap operas. Lots of visitors came by to welcome Emily. It felt warm, familiar.
<v Diane Wu>艾米丽在铁轨旁的一个小房子里，和一个叫东豆川（Dongducheon）的小镇，和她的亲生家庭住了三天。院子里有装满泡菜的陶瓷缸，客厅里有一台总在播放韩国综艺节目和肥皂剧的平板电视。许多游客来看望艾米丽。感觉很温暖，很熟悉。

01:00:52.580 --> 01:01:10.400
<v Diane Wu>On the first night, Emily was getting ready for bed, and her birth mother came in and presented her with four boxes containing gold jewelry for Emily, her kids, and her husband. Emily accepted them, hesitantly. Then using a translation app, her birth mother told her--
<v Diane Wu>第一晚，艾米丽准备睡觉，亲生母亲走进来送给她四个盒子，里面装着给艾米丽、她孩子和她丈夫的金首饰。艾米丽犹豫着接受了。然后使用翻译软件，她的亲生母亲告诉她——

01:01:10.400 --> 01:01:16.280
<v Emily>She said Korean mothers sleep with their children. And I'm like, oh, god, here we go.
<v Emily>她说韩国母亲和孩子一起睡。我心想，噢，天哪，来了。

01:01:16.280 --> 01:01:37.560
<v Diane Wu>Emily had heard about this-- that it's not unusual for Korean parents to sleep in the same room as their kids, even when they're adults. In this room, there was only one bed. She tried to explain to her birth mother, sorry, but I prefer to sleep alone. It wasn't clicking. Emily ended up calling an interpreter she'd hired who had already gone home for the day.
<v Diane Wu>艾米丽听说过这个——韩国父母即使在孩子成年后也和孩子住同一个房间并不罕见。在这个房间里，只有一张床。她试图向亲生母亲解释，抱歉，但我更喜欢一个人睡。这解释不通。艾米丽最终给她雇的一位已经下班的翻译打了电话。

01:01:37.560 --> 01:02:09.420
<v Emily>And so I called her and asked her to explain that I couldn't do it. And she did, but you could see that my birth mother was pissed and not really happy about it. She had missed years and years of my life. I can understand why she'd want to sleep in the same room with me, but I just couldn't do it.
<v Emily>所以我打电话给她请她解释我做不到。她照做了，但你能看出来我亲生母亲很生气，很不高兴。她错过了我生命中好多年。我可以理解为什么她想和我住同一个房间，但我就是做不到。

01:02:09.420 --> 01:02:31.980
<v Diane Wu>A lot of the time with her birth mother was like this. As a visitor, it was impossible to fit in completely. She was a stranger and family all at the same time, dropping into a place that really felt like it could be home-- but it wasn't.
<v Diane Wu>和她亲生母亲在一起的大部分时间都是这样。作为一个访客，完全融入是不可能的。她既是陌生人又是家人，进入一个感觉确实可以成为家的地方——但这并不是家。

01:02:31.980 --> 01:02:44.610
<v Ira Glass>Diane Wu is our show's managing editor. That podcast, Motherhood Sessions, where we first heard Emily's story, it's a show about women and moms in moments of crisis and change. It starts its second season this week.
<v Ira Glass>黛安·吴是我们节目的执行编辑。那个播客《母亲会议》，我们第一次听到艾米丽故事的地方，是一个关于女性和母亲在危机和变化时刻的节目。它本周开始了第二季。

01:02:44.610 --> 01:02:49.050
